\id 1SA - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.2.19
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 1 Samuel
\toc1 1 Samuel
\toc2 1 Samuel
\mt1 1 Samuel
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Elkanah and family at Shiloh
\p
\v 1 There was a man named Elkanah from Ramatayim-Tsofim \add who lived\add* in the Efrati hill country. (He was an Efrati—the son of Yeroham, son of Elihu, son of Tohu, son of Tsuf.)
\v 2 He had two wives: Hannah (who had no children) and Peninah (who had \add several\add* children).
\p
\v 3 Every year he would \add take his family\add* to Shiloh to offer sacrifices to Commander-in-chief Yahweh. Eli was Yahweh’s priest there, and his two sons Hofni and Pinhas \add also served there\add*.
\p
\v 4 on the day that Elkanah made his sacrifice, he would give a helping \add of the cooked meat\add* to Peninah and to all her sons and daughters,
\v 5 but he’d give a double helping to Hannah because he loved her, \add even though\add* Yahweh had not enabled her to have children.
\v 6 Then her rival would provoke her and try to make her miserable, because Yahweh hadn’t given Hannah children.
\v 7 And so it happened every year. Every time they went to Yahweh’s tent, Peninah ridiculed him, and Hannah would cry, and wouldn’t eat.
\v 8 Then Elkanah would ask her, “Hannah, why are you sobbing? And why aren’t you eating? And why are you so upset? Isn’t it better having me than having ten sons?”
\s1 Hannah and Eli
\p
\v 9 \add One time\add* Hannah got up after eating and drinking at Shiloh. (Eli the priest was sitting on the seat near the entrance of Yahweh’s tent.)
\v 10 Hannah was hurting inside, and she prayed to Yahweh and wept uncontrollably.
\v 11 Then she made a vow, saying, “Commander Yahweh, if you would only notice the misery of your female servant and remember me. Don’t ignore me. Help your female servant to give birth to a son, then I’ll give him to you for his entire life, and he’ll never have his hair cut.”
\p
\v 12 As she continued praying to Yahweh, Eli was watching her mouth.
\v 13 Now Hannah was praying from her heart, and her lips were moving but she wasn’t speaking aloud. Eli thought \add she must be\add* drunk,
\v 14 and scolded her, “You shouldn’t be getting drunk. Get rid of your wine.”
\p
\v 15 “No, my master,” Hannah replied, “I’m a woman who’s suffering deep down. I haven’t drunk any wine or intoxicating drink, but rather I’ve been sharing my inner issues with Yahweh.
\v 16 Don’t think that your female servant is \add just\add* a worthless woman, because just now I was praying as a result of my terrible anguish and frustration.”
\p
\v 17 “\add Then\add* go in peace,” Eli replied. “And may Israel’s god grant your request that you presented to him.”
\p
\v 18 “Please assume the best of your female servant,” said Hannah, then she went on her way and had something to eat, and something had changed in her demeanor.
\s1 Samuel’s birth and dedication
\p
\v 19 They \add all\add* gut up early the \add next\add* morning and worshipped Yahweh \add again\add*, then they returned to their home at Ramah. Then Elkanah slept with his wife Hannah, and Yahweh answered her prayer.
\v 20 Hannah got pregnant, and in due course, she gave birth to a son. She named him ‘Samuel’ \add meaning ‘heard by God’, usually changed to ‘Samuel’ in English)\add* because she had requested him from Yahweh.
\p
\v 21 \add At the usual time,\add* Elkanah and all his household went \add to Shiloh\add* to offer their sacrifices and fulfill their vows to Yahweh.
\v 22 But Hannah didn’t accompany them because she’d told her husband, “I’ll \add come and\add* bring him when he’s weaned. Then he can be taken in to Yahweh and permanently remain there.”
\v 23 “Do what you think’s best,” her husband replied. “Stay until you’ve weaned him. May Yahweh do whatever he said \add to you\add*.” So she stayed \add behind\add* and breastfed her son until she eventually weaned him.
\p
\v 24 \add The next year,\add* after she’d weaned him, she took the young boy with them, along with three bulls, a sack of flour, and a container of wine. Then she brought him to Yahweh’s tent in Shiloh,
\v 25 and after slaughtering the bull, they took the boy in to Eli.
\v 26 “Please, my master,” Hannah said. “As your spirit lives, my master, I’m the woman who was standing with you here to pray to Yahweh.
\v 27 I prayed that Yahweh would give me a son, and he did, and this is him.
\v 28 Also, I dedicated him to Yahweh all his life, so he’s now given to Yahweh.”
\p Then they worshipped Yahweh there.
\c 2
\s1 Hannah’s praise
\q1
\v 1 Then Hannah prayed, saying,
\q1 “I’m so happy with Yahweh.
\q1 My strength comes from Yahweh.
\q1 I can loudly refute my enemies.
\q1 Because I’m so pleased that you rescued me.
\b
\q1
\v 2 No one \add else\add* is holy like Yahweh.
\q1 There’s certainly no one else who’s like you.
\q1 No one else \add is like a\add* rock \add to me\add* like our god is.
\q1
\v 3 You all shouldn’t speak so proudly.
\q1 Don’t let arrogance come out of your mouths.
\q1 Because Yahweh is a god of knowledge,
\q2 and all actions are weighed by him.
\b
\q1
\v 4 Warriors have their bows shattered,
\q1 ^but those who stumbled are given extra strength.
\q1
\v 5 Those who had been satisfied \add now\add* hire themselves out for food,
\q2 ^but the hungry people no longer lack.
\q1 The woman who’d been childless, gives birth to seven,
\q2 ^but the woman with many sons is depressed.
\q1
\v 6 Yahweh puts to death and he makes alive.
\q1 He sends down to the grave and brings up.
\q1
\v 7 Yahweh makes poor and makes rich.
\q1 He humbles, and he also honours.
\q1
\v 8 He lifts the poor up from the dust,
\q1 and lifts up the needy from the ash heap
\q1 →so they can sit with influential \add people\add*
\q1 and be given the seat of honour.
\q1 The earth’s foundations belong to Yahweh—
\q1 he was the one who placed the world on top of them.
\q1
\v 9 He watches over those who are loyal to him,
\q1 ^but wicked \add people\add* will die in the darkness,
\q2 because we don’t succeed through our own strength.
\q1
\v 10 It’s Yahweh who shatters our enemies.
\q1 He thunders against them from the heavens.
\q1 Yahweh will judge the entire earth,
\q1 and will give strength to the king,
\q1 and will give victory to his chosen one.”
\p
\v 11 Then Elkanah \add and his family\add* returned to their home in Ramah, but young Samuel \add stayed to\add* help Eli the priest serve Yahweh.
\s1 Eli’s greedy sons
\p
\v 12 Now Eli’s sons were worthless scoundrels who didn’t obey Yahweh.
\v 13 It was the custom of those priests that when anyone was offering a sacrifice and as soon as the meat was boiling, the priest’s servant would come along holding a three-pronged fork
\v 14 and stick it into the pot or pan. The priest would then claim whatever the fork brought up. That’s what they did to all the Israelites who came to Shiloh \add to offer sacrifices\add*.
\v 15 Also, before the fat \add was removed to\add* burn, the priest’s servant would say to the person sacrificing, “Give some meat to the priest—he doesn’t want just boiled meat \add all the time\add*—he’ll take some raw meat \add as well\add* to roast.”
\p
\v 16 But if the person tried to say, “Once the fat has all been burnt, then take whatever you want for yourself,” then he’d assert, “No, you’d better give it \add right\add* now or I’ll take it by force.”
\v 17 And \add so\add* the actions of those young men were very wicked in Yahweh’s mind because they \add just\add* treated sacrifices to Yahweh with contempt.
\s1 Young Samuel starts serving at Shiloh
\p
\v 18 By now Samuel was serving Yahweh, dressed in a priestly uniform made from linen.
\v 19 Each year his mother would make a small robe for him and bring it when she came with her husband to offer their sacrifices.
\v 20 Eli would bless Elkanah and his wife and say, “May Yahweh give more children for you through this woman in place of the \add boy\add* dedicated to Yahweh.” Then they’d return home.
\p
\v 21 So Yahweh visited Hannah and she went on to give birth to three \add other\add* sons and two daughters. Meanwhile, young Samuel grew up serving Yahweh.
\s1 Eli and his children
\p
\v 22 Now Eli was very old and he had heard all about what his sons were doing to the Israelite people, and \add also\add* that they were sleeping around with the women who worked at the \add sacred\add* tent entrance.
\v 23 “Why are you \add two\add* doing all these things that I keep hearing about—these evil things affecting all those people?” he asked them.
\v 24 “No, my sons. The reports that I keep hearing Yahweh’s people passing on aren’t good!
\v 25 If someone sins against a person, then God will mediate for him, but if someone sins against Yahweh, who will speak up for that person?” But they wouldn’t listen to their father’s advice because Yahweh wanted to put them to death.
\p
\v 26 Meanwhile the boy Samuel was growing \add up\add*, and his good \add behaviour pleased\add* both Yahweh and the people.
\s1 The prophecy against Eli’s family
\p
\v 27 One day one of God’s \add prophets\add* came to Eli and told him, “This is what Yahweh says, ‘Didn’t I clearly reveal myself to your ancestors when they were Pharaoh’s slaves in Egypt?
\v 28 And out of all the Israelite tribes, I chose your ancestor to be a priest to me, to go up on my altar, to burn incense, \add and\add* to wear the sacred apron in front of me. Plus I gave your ancestors \add the rights to\add* all the burnt offerings made by the Israelites.
\v 29 Why do you disrespect sacrifices and offerings that I commanded \add the people\add* to bring to me at my residence. And why do you honour your sons more than me by fattening yourselves from the best of every offering of my people Israel?’
\p
\v 30 “Therefore, this is what Yahweh the god of Israel declares: ‘I indeed said your ancestors and then your descendants would serve me forever.’ But now I declare this \add instead\add*: ‘This cannot continue because I will honour those who honour me, but those who despise me will be side-lined.
\v 31 Listen, the time is coming when I’ll bring you and your relatives to an end—none of your men will die of old age
\v 32 and you’ll see distress in my residence. I’ll do good for \add the rest of\add* Israel, but there’ll never be another old man in your home.
\v 33 I won’t finish them off near my altar. Your eyes will fail and you’ll grieve inside, but your \add adult sons\add* will die.
\v 34 This will be the sign: it’ll turn out that your two sons Hofni and Pinhas will both die on the same day.
\v 35 Then I’ll select another man to be a faithful priest to me—he’ll follow my desires and do what’s in my mind. I’ll make him and his faithful descendants to continue and to always serve me.
\v 36 Any of your descendants who remain alive will have to go to him to request food and money and beg for some priestly work to earn something to eat.’ ”
\c 3
\s1 Yahweh calls Samuel
\p
\v 1 Meanwhile young Samuel was serving Yahweh under Eli. In those days, Yahweh rarely spoke to the people or sent visions.
\p
\v 2 By that time, Eli’s sight was very poor—he could barely see. \add One night when\add* he was sleeping in his place,\f + \fr 3:2 \ft Some translations use ‘room’ here instead of ‘place’, but it could easily be that he had a small bed in a corner somewhere.\f*
\v 3 and Samuel was lying down in Yahweh’s tent (where the box was that contained the stone slabs), but God’s lamp was still burning.
\v 4 Yahweh called out to Samuel, and he called back, “I’m here. \add Coming.\add*”
\v 5 Then he ran to Eli and said, “Here I am, because you called me.”
\p “I didn’t call,” he said. “Go back \add and\add* lie down.” So he went \add back\add* and laid down.
\p
\v 6 Yahweh called again, “Samuel.”
\p Samuel got up and went to Eli and said, “Here I am, because you called me.”
\p “I didn’t call, my son,” he said, “Go back \add and\add* lie down.”
\p
\v 7 (\add At that point\add*, Samuel didn’t \add really\add* know Yahweh, because Yahweh hadn’t revealed \add himself\add* to him before.)
\v 8 Then Yahweh called Samuel a third time. He got up and went to Eli and said, “Here I am, because you called me.”
\p Then Eli realised that it was Yahweh who was calling the boy
\v 9 and he told Samuel, “Go \add and\add* lie down. Then if he calls to you \add again\add*, just answer, ‘Speak, Yahweh, because your servant is listening.’ ” So Samuel went and laid down \add again\add* in his place.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh came and stood nearby and called like he’d done the other times, “Samuel, Samuel.”
\p “Speak,” Samuel said, “because your servant is listening.”
\p
\v 11 “Listen,” Yahweh told him, “I’m about to do something in Israel that will make everyone’s ears tingle when they hear it.
\v 12 When it happens, I’ll do everything that I’ve said about Eli and his family, from the beginning to the end.
\v 13 I’ve told him \add already\add* that I’m about to eternally judge his household because he knew about the evil that was being done, because his sons were bringing curses on themselves and he didn’t scold them.
\v 14 That’s why I’ve promised Eli’s family that their disobedience can never be forgiven by means of sacrifices or offerings.
\s1 Samuel tells Eli what Yahweh said
\p
\v 15 Then Samuel lay down \add again\add* until the morning when he opened the doors of Yahweh’s house \add as usual\add*, but he was scared to tell Eli about the vision.
\v 16 But Eli called him, “Samuel, my son.”
\p “Here I am,” he answered.
\p
\v 17 “What did he tell you?” Eli asked. “Don’t hide it from me. May God punish you severely if you hide a \add single\add* word from everything he told you.”
\v 18 So Samuel told him everything without hiding anything. Then Eli answered, “It’s Yahweh. May he do whatever he thinks best.”
\p
\v 19 As Samuel grew up, Yahweh was with him and nothing he said failed to happen,
\v 20 so all Israel from Dan \add in the north\add* and as far as Beersheba \add in the south\add* knew that Samuel was a faithful prophet of Yahweh.
\v 21 \add Over time,\add* Yahweh continued to appear in Shiloh and to reveal his plans through Samuel
\c 4
\nb
\v 1 and Samuel’s messages went to all Israel.
\s1 The capture of the box with the agreement
\p \add At that time,\add* Israel went to war against the Philistines. They camped at Ebenezer, and the Philistines camped at Afek.
\v 2 Then the Philistines lined up to meet Israel and the battle spread. Israel was defeated by the Philistines and they killed about 4,000 warriors in the battle line in the countryside.
\v 3 As the people\f + \fr 4:3 \ft It seems that most translations assume that this is referring to the remainder of the warriors staggering back into the camp (which is certainly a possibility), but we’ve interpreted it as the people coming together (with their elders as it says), to try to figure out why they were defeated. (Similarly for v4.)\f* came into the camp, the Israelite elders asked \add themselves\add*, “Why did Yahweh allow the Philistines to slaughter us today? Let’s get the box from Shiloh that contains \add the stone slabs with\add* Yahweh’s agreement \add on them\add*. Once it’s amongst us, \add surely\add* it’ll save us from defeat by our enemies.”
\v 4 So the people sent men to Shiloh and from there they lifted the box containing Yahweh’s agreement \add and the sacred platform\add* with the winged creatures.\f + \fr 4:4 \ft And with Commander Yahweh sitting on, above, or between them.\f* Eli’s two sons, Hofni and Pinhas, were there with the box containing God’s agreement.
\p
\v 5 As the box with Yahweh’s agreement was carried into the camp, all the Israelites shouted so loud that the ground shook.
\v 6 When the Philistines heard the noise, they said, “What’s that loud shout in the Hebrews’ camp?” Then they realised that the box with Yahweh’s covenant had come into their camp
\v 7 and they were afraid saying, “A god has come into the \add Israelite\add* camp.” Then they said, “This means trouble, because it wasn’t like this yesterday or the previous days.
\v 8 Oh dear! Who will rescue us from these mighty gods? Those are the gods who struck the Egyptians with every plague in the wilderness.
\v 9 \add Now\add* we Philistines really need to fight with passion, because if we don’t, we’ll ending up serving the Hebrews just like they’ve had to serve us. Now, gather your courage and fight for your lives.”
\p
\v 10 So the Philistines fought \add hard\add*, and Israel was defeated. Many warriors fled back to their tents and it was a terrible slaughter—30,000 foot soldiers from Israel were killed.
\v 11 As well as that, the box with God’s \add agreement\add* was taken, and Eli’s two sons Hofni and Pinhas were killed.
\s1 The death of Eli
\p
\v 12 A Benyamite man ran from the battle line back to Shiloh that day. His clothes were torn and he was covered in dirt.
\v 13 When he arrived \add there\add*, Eli was sitting on a roadside seat watching, because he was very concerned about the sacred chest. As the man entered the city with the news, all the people started wailing loudly.
\v 14 As Eli heard the wailing, he asked, “What’s that big commotion?” So the man ran over to Eli to tell him the news.
\v 15 At that time, Eli was ninety-eight years old, and he stared straight ahead because he couldn’t see.
\v 16 The man told him, “I’m the one who’s \add just\add* come from the battle line. I \add had to\add* fled from the battle line today.”
\p “What was the issue, my son?” Eli asked.
\p
\v 17 “Israel fled defeated from the Philistines,” said the messenger. “And also, there has been a terrible slaughter of our people. What’s more, your two sons, Hofni and Pinhas, were killed, and the sacred chest has been captured.”
\p
\v 18 The moment he mentioned the capturing of God’s box, Eli fell backwards off the seat beside the \add city\add* gate. Because he was old and \add very\add* heavy, his neck broke and he died. Eli had led Israel for forty years.
\s1 The death of Pinhas’ widow
\p
\v 19 Now Pinhas’ wife (Eli’s daughter-in-law) was pregnant—about to give birth—when she heard the news about the sacred chest being taken, and that \add both\add* her father-in-law and her husband had died. At that moment, she knelt down and gave birth because the birth pains suddenly started.
\v 20 But she was dying, and the women who stood over her said, “Don’t be afraid, because you’ve given birth to a son.” But she didn’t answer or pay any attention.
\v 21 She named the boy ‘I-Kavod’ \add (which means ‘no glory’)\add*, saying, “The glory has departed from Israel,” about the sacred chest being taken, and about \add the deaths of\add* her father-in-law and her husband.
\v 22 Then she said, “The glory has departed from Israel, because the sacred chest has been taken.”
\c 5
\s1 The sacred chest gets carried to Ashdod
\p
\v 1 Now the Philistines had taken the sacred chest, and they took it from Ebenezer to Ashdod
\v 2 where they carried it into the temple of \add their god\add* Dagon and placed it beside \add a statue of\add* Dagon.
\v 3 Early the next day when the Ashdodim got up, to their horror Dagon had fallen to the ground on its face in front of Yahweh’s box. So they stood it up and returned it to its place.
\v 4 \add But\add* then the next morning, more shock: Dagon had fallen to the ground on its face in front of Yahweh’s box but now its hands and head were cut off, \add and were lying\add* on the threshold. Only its body remained intact.
\v 5 (Because of that, until this day Dagon’s priests and everyone who enters Dagon’s temple in Ashdod won’t tread on the threshold.)
\s1 God punishes the Philistine cities
\p
\v 6 Yahweh caused a lot of trouble for the \add people of\add* Ashdod and he terrified them. He caused them and the people in the region to get lumpy growths on their skin.
\v 7 When the leaders in Ashdod saw what was happening, they said, “The god of Israel’s box mustn’t stay with us because he’s punishing \add both\add* us and our god Dagon.”
\v 8 So they sent for all the Philistine rulers, and \add when they’d assembled\add* they asked, “What should we do with the god of Israel’s box?”
\p “Let the god of Israel’s box be taken around to Gath,” they decided. So they moved it \add there\add*.
\v 9 After it arrived, Yahweh started punishing Gath and \add both\add* young and old men in the city started getting growths on their skin, and it caused a huge panic.
\v 10 So they sent God’s box to Ekron, but as soon as it entered the city, the Ekronim cried out, “\add Hey!\add* They’ve brought the god of Israel’s box here to kill us and our people.”
\v 11 So they sent for the Philistine rulers, and when they’d assembled they said, “Send the god of Israel’s box away. Send it back to its place so it won’t cause us and our people to die.” Because there was a deadly panic all over the city \add as\add* God’s punishment there was severe
\v 12 and the men who didn’t die suffered with the growths. So the people’s cry went up to the heavens.
\c 6
\s1 Sending the chest back to Israel
\p
\v 1 So it was that Yahweh’s box was \add held\add* in Philistine territory for seven months,
\v 2 then the Philistines called their priests and diviners and asked, “What should we do with Yahweh’s box? Show us how we can get it back away to its place.”
\p
\v 3 “If you’re going to send the god of Israel’s box back,” they responded, “don’t send it back by itself, but definitely return it to him with a guilt offering. Then you’ll all be healed, and you’ll all understand why you’ve been suffering.”
\p
\v 4 “What guilt offering should we send with it?” they asked.
\p “Five gold \add replicas of the\add* tumours, and five gold mice,” they said. “That’s the number of our rulers, because the same plague affected both them and the people.
\v 5 Make replicas of the tumours and replicas of the mice that have been destroying the land, and it will honour Israel’s god. Then perhaps he will less his punishment that’s been on you and on your gods and on your land.
\v 6 Why have you all been so stubborn just like the Egyptians when Pharaoh made them stubborn? When Israel’s god dealt severely with them, didn’t the Egyptians send the Israelites away and they went?
\v 7 So now, get a brand new cart with two nursing cows which have never had a yoke on their necks. Hitch the cows to the cart but take their calves back to their pen.
\v 8 Then pick up Yahweh’s box and put it into the cart along with the gold objects that you are sending with it as a guilt offering put in another container beside the box. Finally, send the cart away and \add off\add* it will go.
\v 9 Then watch it. If it heads towards Beyt-Shemesh in Israelite territory, then it’s their god that caused this terrible calamity. But if not, then we’ll know that it wasn’t the Israelite god, and that it \add all\add* happened to us by chance.”
\p
\v 10 So they followed those instructions: they took two cows that had been feeding calves and hitched them to the \add new\add* cart, but they shut their calves away in the stall.
\v 11 Then they put Yahweh’s box into the cart with the \add other\add* container with the gold mice and the replicas of their tumours.
\v 12 \add Amazingly,\add* the cows went straight down the road going to Beyt-Shemesh. They stayed on that one highway, walking and mooing, and they never once deviated to the left or the right, and the Philistine rulers followed them as far as the border of Beyt-Shemesh.
\p
\v 13 Meanwhile in Beyt-Shemesh, \add the people\add* were harvesting wheat in the valley, and when they looked up and saw the box, they were very happy.
\v 14 The cart came into the field belonging to Joshua and \add then the cows just\add* stood there. There was a large stone there, and the people split \add the sides of\add* the wooden cart \add into pieces to start a fire\add*, then offered the cows as a burnt sacrifice to Yahweh.
\v 15 Then the Levites lifted down Yahweh’s box and the \add other\add* container that was with it with the gold objects in it, and they placed them on the large stone. Then the men of Beyt-Shemesh offered up \add more\add* burnt offerings and sacrificed sacrifices to Yahweh on that day.
\v 16 The five Philistine rulers watched \add all that from a distance before\add* returning to Ekron that same day.
\s1 The gold replicas
\p
\v 17 The gold \add replicas\add* of tumours that the Philistines sent back as a guilt offering to Yahweh were for \add the five cities\add*: Ashdod, Gaza, Ashkelon, Gath, and Ekron.
\v 18 And the gold mice were from the number of all the Philistine cities under the five rulers, including fortified cities as well as the villages in the open country, and as far as the large stone that they sat Yahweh’s box on. (It’s \add still\add* in Joshua’s field in Beyt-Shemesh until this day.)
\p
\v 19 But some Beyt-Shemesh men looked into Yahweh’s box, and he killed seventy\f + \fr 6:19 \ft TC: The oldest Hebrew text has 50,070 men being killed here, making it hard to imagine how that many men kept clambering over dead bodies to look into the sacred chest. (But possibly we’re not understanding the situation correctly?) We have followed later manuscripts here.\f* of them. The people mourned because Yahweh had caused such devastation among the people.
\s1 The sacred chest is taken to Kiryat-Yearim
\p
\v 20 The Beyt-Shemesh men said, “Who can stand in front of the face of Yahweh, this holy God? And where should we send the box to from here?”
\v 21 So they sent messengers to those living in Kiryat-Yearim, saying, “The Philistines have returned Yahweh’s box. Come over here and get it.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 So the men from Kiryat-Yearim came and lifted up Yahweh’s box and brought it into Avinadav’s house on the hill. They consecrated his son Eleazar to take care of it.
\s1 Samuel guides Israel
\p
\v 2 The sacred chest ended up staying in Kiryat-Yearim for twenty years but the Israelites longed sadly for Yahweh.
\p
\v 3 Then Samuel told all the Israelites, “If you all \add really want\add* to return to Yahweh, remove the foreign gods and the Ashtarot from among you. Then decide firmly to serve Yahweh and no other, then he’ll rescue you from the Philistines’ oppression.”
\v 4 So the Israelites removed the Baal and Ashtoret \add idols\add*, and \add began to\add* serve Yahweh only.
\p
\v 5 Then Samuel called, “Gather all Israel to Mitspah, and I’ll pray to Yahweh for you all.”
\v 6 So they gathered at Mitspah, and drew water and poured it out in front of Yahweh, and they fasted on that day and said there, “We have sinned against Yahweh.” And Samuel guided the Israelites at Mitspah.
\p
\v 7 When the Philistines heard that the Israelites had gathered at Mitspah, the Philistine rulers decided to attack Israel. When the Israelites heard that, they were afraid of the Philistines
\v 8 and begged Samuel, “Don’t stop crying out to our god Yahweh, so that he’ll rescue us from the Philistines.”
\v 9 So Samuel got a young lamb and offered it up—a whole burnt up offering to Yahweh. And he cried out to Yahweh for Israel, and Yahweh answered him.
\v 10 While Samuel was offering up that burnt offering, the Philistines approached to battle against Israel. But that day Yahweh made an intense sound like thunder that confused the Philistines and the Israelites were able to defeat them.
\v 11 The Israelite men ran out from Mitspah and chased the Philistines—killing them all the way to Beyt-Kar.
\p
\v 12 Samuel took a single rock and stood it between Mitspah and Shen. He named it ‘Ebenezer’ \add which means ‘stone of help’)\add*, saying, “Yahweh helped us all the way up to here.”
\v 13 So the Philistines ended up being subdued and didn’t invade Israel’s border again, and Yahweh worked against the Philistines as long as Samuel lived.
\v 14 The cities that the Philistines had taken from Ekron and as far as Gath were returned to Israel. And \add so\add* Israel rescued their territory from the Philistines, and there was peace between Israel and the Amorites.
\p
\v 15 So Samuel guided Israel while he lived.
\v 16 Every year he did the circuit around Bethel, Gilgal, and Mitspah, and he helped settled disputes for the people in all those places,
\v 17 but he \add always\add* returned to Ramah because his home was there. He would judge disputes there, and built an altar to Yahweh there.
\c 8
\s1 The Israelites request a king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 As Samuel grew older, he appointed his \add two\add* sons as judges over Israel.
\v 2 The eldest was Joel and his brother was Abijah. They were based in Beersheba.
\v 3 However, they didn’t take after time, but rather chased dishonest gain—accepting bribes and perverting justice.
\p
\v 4 Eventually, the Israelite elders met together with Samuel at Ramah
\v 5 and requested, “Listen, you’re old \add now\add*, and your sons don’t follow your example. So appoint a king for us now to lead us like all the other nations.”
\v 6 But Samuel thought that their request for a king was an evil request, so he prayed to Yahweh
\v 7 who replied, “Listen to everything that the people are saying, because they’re not rejecting \em you\em*, but they’re rejecting me from reigning over them.
\v 8 They’re doing to you just what they’ve done to me every since I rescued them out of Egypt and right up until today: deserting me and serving other gods.
\v 9 So listen to what they’re saying now, but certainly warn them about what kings require of their subjects.”
\s1 Advance warnings about having a king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then Samuel passed on everything that Yahweh had said to the people who were requesting a king from him,
\v 11 telling them, “If you all have a king reigning over you, this is what he’ll demand: he will conscript your sons and appoint them as his cavalry or charioteers, or they’ll run as foot-soldiers in front, of his chariot.
\v 12 Others he’ll appoint a commander of various units. Some will end up ploughing his fields or harvesting his crops, or manufacturing his weapons and chariots.
\v 13 He’ll take your daughters to manufacture perfumes, or to be cooks and bakers.
\v 14 He’ll confiscate your best fields and vineyards and olive orchards, and give to his servants \add to control\add*.
\v 15 Then he’ll demand a tenth of your seeds and grape harvests for his officials and his servants.
\v 16 He’ll commandeer \add some of\add* your best male and female servants and young men, and he’ll impound \add some of\add* your donkeys, then he’ll use \add them all\add* for his projects.
\v 17 He’ll expect a tenth of your sheep and goats, and you yourselves will end up being his slaves.
\v 18 When that all happens, you’ll all cry out in front of the king who you chose for yourselves, but Yahweh won’t be answering you at that time.”
\p
\v 19 However, the people refused to consider Samuel’s advice and they argued, “No, we want a king over us
\v 20 so we’ll be like all the other nations. Our king will judge us, and will lead out our warriors in battle.”
\v 21 Samuel listened to everything the people said, and passed it on to Yahweh,
\v 22 and Yahweh told him, “Do what they’re asking and get them a king.” So Samuel \add told the people and then sent them all back to their homes\add*.
\c 9
\s1 Saul is chosen as king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now there was a Benyamite man named Kish (son of Aviel, son of Tseror, son of Bekorat, son of Afiah, the son of a Yemini man) who was strong and wealthy.
\v 2 Kish had a son named Saul who was a handsome young man and good—in fact there wasn’t any Israelite man better than him, \add and\add* he was head and shoulders taller than anyone else.
\p
\v 3 One time, the female donkeys of Saul’s father Kish had gone missing, and Kish told his son, “Take one of the servants with you, and pack up and go and find those donkeys.”
\v 4 So they passed through the Efrati hill country, then through the Shalishah region, but they didn’t find them. Then they went through the Sha’alim region, but there was nothing, so they continued on through the land of the Benyamites, but they didn’t find them.
\v 5 \add Finally,\add* they entered the Tsuf area, and \add eventually\add* Saul said to his servant, “Come on. Let’s go back home in case my father stops worrying about the donkeys and \add starts\add* worrying about us.”
\p
\v 6 But he replied, “Listen, please. There’s an honoured man of God in this city. Everything he says comes true. Let’s go there now. Perhaps he’ll tell us which way we should have gone.”
\p
\v 7 “Okay, then. We can go \add there\add*,” Saul responded, “but what can we give the man? We don’t have any more food in our bags, and we never brought along a gift. What else do we have?”
\p
\v 8 “Look,” answered the servant, “I’ve got a small silver coin. We can give that to the man and he’ll tell us where to go.”
\v 9 (In Israel before then, when people wanted an answer from God, they’d say, “Come and let us go to the ‘seer’,” because the prophet of today was called the ‘seer’ before.)
\p
\v 10 “Good idea,” Saul told his servant. “Let’s go then.” So they went to the city there where the man of God was.
\v 11 They were climbing up the rise towards the city when they met some young women going out to draw water, and they asked them, “Is the seer in town?”
\v 12 “Yes he is,” they answered. “Hurry though, because he’s come to the city today \add to offer\add* the sacrifice for the people at the high place.
\v 13 You’ll find him as soon as you enter the city, before he goes up to the high place to eat. The people won’t eat until he gets there, because he himself will bless the sacrifice. Afterwards, those who’ve been invited will eat \add together there\add*. So go up there now and you’ll soon find him.”
\v 14 So they went on up into the city, and as they were entering, wow, there was Samuel coming out towards them to go up to the high place.
\p
\v 15 On the previous day, Yahweh had quietly told Samuel,
\v 16 “About this time tomorrow, I’ll send a Benyamite man to you, and you should anoint him to be ruler over my people Israel. He will rescue my people from the Philistines, because I’ve noticed my people because I heard their cries.”
\p
\v 17 Then Samuel saw Saul, and Yahweh told him, “Look, \add that’s\add* the man that I you \add about\add*. He will \add help control\add* my people.”
\v 18 As Saul approached Samuel right there in the middle of the city gateway, he asked, “Please tell me, where’s the seer’s house?”
\p
\v 19 “I am the seer,” Samuel answered, “\add Now,\add* Go up to the high place ahead of me, and you’ll eat with me today. In the morning, I’ll tell you what you want to know, then I’ll send you off.
\v 20 As for your donkeys that were lost three days ago, don’t worry anymore about them because they’ve been found. Now, all of Israel is wanting your father’s household, and wanting you \add in particular\add*.”
\v 21 “But I’m a Benyamite,” Saul responded, “\add That’s\add* the smallest tribe in Israel, and my clan’s the lowliest of all the Benyamite clans. So why did you talk to me like that?”
\p
\v 22 Then Samuel took Saul and his servant to the room \add where the meal had been prepared\add*, and he gave them a place at the head of table—even above the invited guests. (There were about thirty men.)
\v 23 And Samuel told the cook, “Give the \add special\add* piece of meat that I showed you and said to keep aside.”
\v 24 So the cook lifted the cooked leg and placed it in front of Saul. Then Samuel told \add Saul\add*, “Look, this has been reserved for you. Eat it, because it’s been kept until the appointed time when I could call these people together.”
\s1 Samuel anoints Saul with oil
\s4 Full kingdom
\p So Saul ate with Samuel that day,
\v 25 and then they went \add back\add* from the high place down into the city, and Samuel spoke with Saul on \add his house\add* roof.
\p
\v 26 They rose early \add the next day\add*. At dawn, Samuel had called to Saul on the roof, saying, “Get up and I will send you off.” So Saul got up, and the two of them went outside.
\v 27 \add When\add* they reached the edge of the city, Samuel said to Saul, “Tell your servant to go on ahead,” and \add so\add* he went on ahead. “But you stand here, and I’ll give you the message from God.”
\c 10
\p
\v 1 Then Samuel took a flask of oil, and poured it on Saul’s head and kissed him, and told him, “It’s Yahweh who has anointed you to be ruler over his chosen people.
\v 2 After you leave me today \add and get back to\add* Benyamite territory, then you’ll see two men near Rachel’s tomb in Tseltsah, and they’ll say to you, ‘The donkeys that you went to look for have been found. But, wow, your father has stopped worrying about the donkeys and \add now\add* he’s worried about \em you\em* and trying to figure out what he should do.’
\v 3 Then further on from there, as you approach the oak tree at Tavor, you’ll find three men going to Bethel to worship God. One will be taking three young goats, one will be carrying three rounds loaves of bread, and one will be carrying a wine in a container.
\v 4 They’ll ask about how you’re doing and give you two loaves of bread which you should accept.
\v 5 After that, you’ll come to the hill of God (where there’s a camp of Philistine \add warriors\add*). When you enter the town, you’ll meet a group of prophets descending from the altar area,
\v 6 and Yahweh’s spirit will rush onto you. Then you’ll \add join\add* them in prophesying, and you’ll be transformed into a different person.
\v 7 When that all comes true, do whatever you think is correct because God will be helping you.
\v 8 Then go ahead of me to Gilgal and wait \add there\add* for seven days, until I get there and tell you what to do. I’ll join you to offer burnt offerings \add and\add* to sacrifice peace offerings.
\p
\v 9 Then as Saul started to leave Samuel, God changed his thinking. Then all Samuel’s predictions came true that day,
\v 10 so when Saul and his servant came to the hill, a group of prophets met them and God’s spirit rushed onto Saul and he joined them in prophesying.
\v 11 Then everyone who had known Saul previously heard him prophesying with the prophets, they asked each other, “What’s happened to Kish’s son? Is Saul really a prophet \add now\add*?”
\v 12 A man from there answered, “And who is \em their\em* father?” So after that it became a common saying, “Has Saul also become a prophet?”
\v 13 When he had finished prophesying, he ascended to the altar area.
\p
\v 14 \add Later,\add* Saul’s uncle asked him and his servant, “Where did you \add two\add* take off to?”
\p “To look for the donkeys,” Saul replied. “But we couldn’t find them, so we went to Samuel.”
\p
\v 15 “And what did he tell you?” the uncle asked.
\p
\v 16 “He assured us that the donkeys had been found,” Saul replied. But he didn’t tell him anything that Samuel had said about becoming king.
\s1 Saul is selected as king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 17 Then Samuel summoned the people \add to come before\add* Yahweh at Mitspah,
\v 18 and he told them, “Yahweh, the god of Israel, says, ‘I myself brought Israel out of Egypt. I rescued you from Egyptian control, and from all the kingdoms that oppressed you.’
\v 19 But since then, you all have rejected your god—the one who saves you from all your calamities and distresses. And you’ve all said to him, ‘We’d rather have a king over us.’ So station yourselves now in front of Yahweh by your tribes and clans.”
\p
\v 20 Then Samuel had all the Israelite tribes approach, and the Benjamin tribe was selected.
\v 21 Then he had all the Benyamite clans approach, and the Matri clan was selected. Then Kish’s son, Saul was selected and they tried to find him, but they couldn’t find him anywhere
\v 22 so they asked Yahweh again, “Is that man around here anywhere?”
\p “Look, he’s hiding among the supplies,” Yahweh replied.
\p
\v 23 So they ran and got him from there, and \add when\add* he took his place among the people, he was head and shoulders taller than anyone else.
\v 24 Then Samuel announced to everyone, “Can you see the one that Yahweh has chosen? Certainly there’s no one else like him among all the people.”
\p All the people saw that and responded, “Long live the king.”
\p
\v 25 Then Samuel explained how the kingship would work, and wrote it in a book and placed it in front of Yahweh. Then he sent everyone home.
\v 26 Samuel also went home to Giveah, accompanied by some of the powerful warriors whose hearts God had touched.
\v 27 But some worthless men asked, “How can that one save us?” and they despised Saul and didn’t bring him any gifts, but he made no comment.
\c 11
\s1 Saul rescues Yavesh from the Ammonites
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add About a month later,\add*\f + \fr 11:1 \ft This appears only in a few ancient documents.\f* Nahash the Ammonite \add took his men\add* and camped around Yavesh-Gilead, and the leaders of Yavesh told him, “Make a treaty with us, and we’ll serve you.”
\p
\v 2 “\add Yes,\add* I’ll make an treaty with you,” Nahash replied, “by gouging out all of your right eyes in order to shame all Israel.”
\p
\v 3 “\add Oh! Then\add* leave us alone for seven days,” the Yavesh elders responded, “so that we can send messengers through all Israel’s territory. If no one will come to rescue us, then we’ll surrender to you.”
\p
\v 4 When the messengers arrived where Saul lived at Giveah and passed the news onto the people, everyone started wailing loudly.
\v 5 At that time, Saul was walking home behind the cattle and he asked, “What’s with \add all\add* the people wailing?” Then they told him what the messengers from Yavesh had said.
\v 6 God’s spirit rushed on Saul as he heard the message, and he became very angry.
\v 7 Then he took a pair of cattle and chopped them into pieces, and sent messengers to take them throughout Israel and announce, “This is what will be done to the cattle of anyone who doesn’t join Saul and Samuel \add in battle\add*.”
\p Then Yahweh caused the people to be afraid of him, and they united together.
\v 8 When Saul assembled them at Bezek, there were three hundred thousand \add warriors\add* from Israel and thirty thousand from Judah,
\v 9 and they told the \add original\add* messengers, “\add Go and\add* tell the leaders of Yavesh-Gilead, ‘Tomorrow you’ll all be rescued by the time that the sun’s at its hottest.’ ”
\v 10 So the Yavesh leaders told \add Nahash\add*, “We’ll surrender to you all tomorrow, then you can do to us whatever seems good to you all.”
\p
\v 11 The next day, Saul divided his warriors into three divisions, and they attacked the Ammonite camp before the sun rose. Then they slaughtered the Ammonites until the hottest part of the day. Their remaining warriors were scattered so completely that no two of them were able to stay together.
\s1 Saul reaffirmed as king
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 12 Then the people asked Samuel, “Who were the ones saying that Saul wasn’t fit to reign over us? Bring them here and we’ll execute them.”
\p
\v 13 “No one’s going to be executed today,” Saul said, “because today Yahweh has rescued Israel.”
\p
\v 14 “Come \add on then\add*,” said Samuel. “Let’s go to Gilgal and renew the kingship there.”
\v 15 So all the people when to Gilgal and in front of Yahweh they reaffirmed Saul as king. Then they offered sacrifices \add as\add* peace offerings to Yahweh, then Saul and the people celebrated together.
\c 12
\s1 Samuel’s farewell speech
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Samuel said to all Israel, “Listen to me now. I’ve listened to your voices and taken notice of everything that you all said to me. And I’ve appointed a king to reign over you all.
\v 2 So look now and you can see your king walking here in front of you all. But as for me, I’m old and grey, and my sons are among you all. I’ve served you all openly from my youth right up to the present time,
\v 3 \add and\add* here I am now. Now with Yahweh listening, answer this truthfully: Have I \add ever\add* taken anyone’s ox or donkey? Did I cheat anyone? Have I oppressed anyone or taken a bribe to not see something? I’ll pay back anything I owe \add anyone\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “\add No,\add* you haven’t cheated us,” they answered. “And you haven’t oppressed us or taken any payments.”
\p
\v 5 “Yahweh is a your witness,” he said. “And his anointed \add king\add* is a witness today, that you have never found me taking anything that wasn’t mine.”
\p “We witness that,” they agreed.
\p
\v 6 “It was Yahweh who appointed Moses and Aaron,” Samuel continued, “and who brought your ancestors out of Egypt.
\v 7 Now present yourselves to Yahweh as I confront you all in front of him \add by telling you about\add* his righteousness that he displayed to you all as well as to your ancestors \add before you\add*:
\v 8 After Jacob went to Egypt and your ancestors cried out to Yahweh, then Yahweh sent Moses and Aaron, and they brought your ancestors out of Egypt and settled them into this place.
\v 9 Then they forgot their god Yahweh, and he allowed them to be oppressed by Sisera (the commander of the army from Hatsor), and the Philistines, and the king of Moab. Then they fought against them
\v 10 and cried out to Yahweh, ‘We have sinned, because we’ve abandoned Yahweh and have served the Baals and the Ashtarot. But if you rescue us now from our enemies, we will serve you.’
\v 11 Then Yahweh sent Gideon, Bedan, Jephthah, and Samson\f + \fr 12:11 \ft TC: This list of four names varies between different ancient manuscripts so you might find variation in other translations.\f* to rescue you all from your enemies all around you, and \add so\add* you all have had times of living securely.
\v 12 But then you all saw Ammonite King Nahash coming against you, and you demanded that you all wanted a king to reign over you rather than having your god Yahweh as your king.
\p
\v 13 So now, look, \add here’s\add* the king that you all chose to have. Yes, Yahweh has given you all a king.
\v 14 If you all honour Yahweh and listen to his instructions and don’t rebel against his commands, then \add both\add* you and your king will \add indeed\add* be following your god Yahweh.
\v 15 But if you all don’t listen to Yahweh, and you rebel against his commands, then he will work against you all just like he worked against your ancestors.\f + \fr 12:15 \ft TC: or (in the ancient Septuagint translation), …against you all \+bd and your king\+bd*.\f*
\v 16 So now stay there and you’ll all see the incredible sign that Yahweh is about to do in front of you.
\v 17 \add Now,\add* isn’t it \add the time of\add* the wheat harvest right now? I’ll call to Yahweh, and he’ll send \add unseasonal\add* thunder and rain, and then you’ll all see and realise that in Yahweh’s view, asking for a king was very evil.”
\p
\v 18 So Samuel called to Yahweh and he sent thunder and rain that day, and all the people became very afraid of Yahweh and Samuel
\v 19 and begged Samuel, “Pray to your god Yahweh for your servants, so that we won’t die, because we’ve added \add more\add* evil onto all our \add previous\add* sins by requesting a king for ourselves.”
\p
\v 20 “Don’t be afraid,” Samuel responded. “You yourselves have \add indeed\add* done all this evil. However, don’t \add make it worse by\add* turning away from Yahweh, but rather, serve him with all your being.
\v 21 And don’t turn away from Yahweh \add and worship\add* useless \add idols\add* because they can’t benefit you or rescue you, because they’re empty \add of life\add*.
\v 22 Yahweh won’t abandon his people because he has an excellent reputation \add to uphold\add*, especially since he’s declared you all to be his people.
\v 23 Also, far be it from me to sin against Yahweh by not praying for you all, as well as instructing you all in the good and proper way.
\v 24 Just honour Yahweh and serve him faithfully and sincerely—\add always\add* remembering the incredible things he’s done for you all.
\v 25 But if you all continue to do evil, both you and your king will get swept away.”
\c 13
\s1 Samuel scolds Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Saul was thirty\f + \fr 13:1 \ft TC: There’s some confusion around the numbers in this verse in the original manuscripts, so other translations might differ.\f* years old when he \add began to\add* reign over Israel, and \add when\add* he’d reigned for two years,
\v 2 he chose three thousand Israelite \add warriors\add* for himself: two thousand were \add stationed\add* with him in Mikmas, and one thousand were with \add his son\add* Jonathan in Benyamite Giveah. All the others, he sent back to their homes.
\p
\v 3 Then Jonathan attacked and defeated the Philistine unit that was at Geva, and the Philistines heard \add about it\add*. So Saul \add had\add* a trumpet blown throughout the country, with the message, “Listen all you Hebrews.”
\v 4 All of Israel heard the news that Saul had wiped out a Philistine outpost, and \add realised that\add* Israel had become repulsive to the Philistines. So the people were summoned to join Saul at Gilgal.
\p
\v 5 The Philistines assembled themselves to fight Israel \add with\add* thirty thousand chariots, six thousand mounted cavalry, and warriors as numerous as the sand on the beach. They went to Mikmas and camped \add there\add*, east of Beyt-Aven.
\v 6 But the Israelite warriors were very anxious because their troops were so outnumbered, so they hid in caves and thickets, among rocks, and in tombs and wells.
\v 7 Some of them crossed the Jordan \add river\add* to the Gad and Gilead regions.
\p Saul stayed at Gilgal, but the people with him were trembling.
\v 8 He waited seven days until the time when Samuel had said that he’d get there, but he didn’t appear so Saul’s men began to desert him.
\v 9 “Bring me the burnt offering and the peace offerings,” Saul commanded, then he offered up the burnt offering.
\v 10 Just as he’d finished, wow, Samuel arrived \add at last\add*, and Saul went over to greet him.
\v 11 “What have you done?” Samuel asked.
\p “When I saw the people leaving me,” Saul replied “and you, you didn’t get here when you said you would, and the Philistines were gathering at Mikmas,
\v 12 then I thought, ‘The Philistines are about to attack us at Gilgal, and I haven’t offered any gift to Yahweh.’ So I forced myself, and offered up the burnt up offering.”
\p
\v 13 “That was stupid of you,” Samuel responded. “You haven’t obeyed what your god Yahweh instructed, because \add if you had,\add* then Yahweh would have established your reign over Israel forever.
\v 14 But now your kingdom won’t survive. Yahweh has found a man who’s loyal to him and he’s commanded that man to lead his people, because you haven’t obeyed him.
\p
\v 15 Then Samuel left Gilgal and went to Giveah in Benyamite \add territory\add*. Meanwhile, Saul counted up the warriors with him—around six hundred \add of them\add*.
\v 16 Saul and his son Jonathan and the people with them, were staying in Geva, whereas the Philistines were camped at Mikmas,
\v 17 and they sent out three raiding parties—one went \add north\add* to Ofrah in the Shual region,
\v 18 one went \add west\add* to Beyt-Horon, and one went towards the wilderness where the border overlooks the Tsevoeim valley.
\p
\v 19 \add At that time,\add* there were no blacksmiths in Israel because the Philistines didn’t want the Israelites to be able to make swords or spears,
\v 20 so all the Israelites had to go to the Philistines to get their axes and plough blades, etc., sharpened.
\v 21 (They were charged two-thirds of a shekel for larger things like plough blades, and one-third for picks and axes, and for straightening \add ox\add* goads.)
\v 22 So on the day of battle, Saul and his son Jonathan had a sword and spear each, but none of their warriors had any.
\p
\v 23 Now one Philistine unit had been sent out to guard the pass at Mikmas.
\c 14
\s1 Jonathan’s bravery
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then one day, Saul’s son Jonathan said to the young man who carried his equipment, “Come on, let’s cross over to the Philistine unit that’s over on the opposite side.” But he didn’t tell his father.
\v 2 Meanwhile, Saul was still waiting under the pomegranate tree in Migron on the outskirts of Geva with around six-hundred warriors.
\v 3 Ahiyah \add the priest\add* was also there wearing a sacred apron. (He was son of Ahituv the brother of I-Kavod, son of Pinhas, son of Eli, Yahweh’s priest at Shiloh.) But the people didn’t know that Jonathan had gone.
\p
\v 4 Meanwhile, for Jonathan to get to the Philistine garrison, they had to go through a narrow pass between two cliffs. (The cliff on one side was named Botsets, and the other was named Seneh.
\v 5 One cliff faced north toward Mikmas, and the other side faced south toward Geva.)
\p
\v 6 Jonathan said to his servant carrying his equipment, “Come on. Let’s pass over to that group of these uncircumcised ones. Perhaps Yahweh will help us, because it doesn’t matter to him whether he saves using many \add people\add* or \add just\add* a few.”
\p
\v 7 “Do everything that you feel is right,” he responded. “Go on ahead and be confident that I’ll be there supporting you.”
\p
\v 8 “Now listen,” Jonathan said. “We’ll cross over and then reveal ourselves to them.
\v 9 Then if they tell us, ‘Stay there until we get down to you,’ then we’ll stay where we are and not go up to them.
\v 10 But if they say, ‘Come on up to us,’ then we’ll go up, because that’ll be the sign to us that Yahweh will help us overpower them.”
\p
\v 11 So the two of them revealed themselves to the Philistine unit, and the soldiers said, “Hey, look! The Hebrews are coming out from the holes there where they’ve hidden themselves.”
\v 12 Then \add some\add* of the men called to Jonathan and his servant, “Come on up here to us and we’ll teach you a thing or two.”
\p “Come on up behind me,” Jonathan told his servant, “because Yahweh has already declared Israel to be the victor.”
\v 13 So Jonathan climbed up on his hands and feet, with his servant following behind him. Then \add at the top\add*, Jonathan struck them down \add with his sword\add* while his servant followed behind and killed them.
\v 14 In that first offensive, Jonathan and his servant killed about twenty men in an area of around 30m square.
\v 15 At that time, \add Philistines\add* in the camp started getting the jitters, then all the people, and \add even\add* raiding parties. Then the ground shook, and it turned into a god-given panic.
\s1 Israel defeats the Philistines
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 16 Saul’s lookouts in Giveah (in Benyamite \add territory\add*) saw to their surprise that their opponents were starting to scatter in every direction.
\v 17 “Count our people and see who’s missing,” Saul commanded, so they called the roll and wow, it was Jonathan and his servant who weren’t accounted for.
\v 18 “Bring the sacred chest here,” Saul told Ahiyah \add the priest\add*, because the Israelites had control of it again at that time.
\v 19 But even as Saul was speaking to him, the confusion in the Philistine camp was continually increasing, and Saul told him, “\add Oh,\add* don’t worry about it.”
\v 20 Then he called his warriors together and they went \add forward\add* to battle. To their surprise, the Philistines were fighting each other in total confusion.
\v 21 Now before that time, some Hebrews had joined the Philistine camp, but now they switched alliance back to Israel with Saul and Jonathan.
\v 22 \add Also,\add* there had been some Israelite deserters who’d hidden in the Efrati hill country heard that the Philistines were fleeing, and \add so\add* they too also chased and attacked them.
\v 23 So Yahweh saved Israel that day, and the fighting went as far as Beyt-Aven.
\s1 Jonathan eats honey
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 24 Now the Israelite warriors were getting exhausted that day, but Saul had made the them promise, “We need to avenge our enemies. Anyone who eats food before the evening is cursed.”
\v 25 But when they reached the forest, there was honeycomb on the ground.
\v 26 Although the warriors entered the forest and saw the honey, none of them tasted any because they were afraid of the curse.
\v 27 But Jonathan hadn’t heard his father’s oath, so he dipped his staff into the honeycomb and ate some honey and felt rejuvenated.
\v 28 However, someone noticed it and mentioned, “Your father made the people promise, ‘Anyone who eats food today will be cursed.’ So the people are faint.”
\p
\v 29 “My father’s caused trouble for us,” Jonathan responded. “See how much better I feel now because I tasted a little \add bit\add* of this honey.
\v 30 Actually, if the troops had eaten what they’d found from the plunder of their enemies today, how much better it would have been because we might have had an even more decisive victory over the Philistines.”
\p
\v 31 That day they killed Philistines from Mikmas to Ayalon, but the fighters were totally exhausted.
\v 32 They rushed greedily at the plunder, grabbing sheep, cattle, and calves which they slaughtered right there and ate with the blood \add still in them\add*.
\v 33 Then \add King\add* Saul was told, “Look, the people are sinning against Yahweh by eating \add meat\add* with the blood \add still in it\add*.”
\p “You’ve all acted treacherously,” he responded, “Now, roll a big stone \add over\add* to me.”
\v 34 Then he said, “Go out among the warriors and tell them to bring their animals over here to slaughter and to eat, so they don’t sin against Yahweh by eating \add meat with\add* the blood \add still in it\add*.” So the people brought their animals over and slaughtered them there.
\v 35 Then Saul built an altar to Yahweh—it was the first one he built.
\p
\v 36 Then Saul said, “Let’s go after the Philistines tonight and plunder them until the light of morning. Let’s not leave any of them alive.”
\p “Do what you feel is right,” they responded.
\p But the priest said, “Let’s ask God here first.”
\p
\v 37 So Saul asked God, “Should we go after the Philistines? Will you give Israel victory over them?” But Yahweh didn’t answer him that day.
\v 38 Then Saul commanded, “All you leaders of the warriors. Gather here so we can find out what sin was committed today.
\v 39 As Yahweh who saves Israel lives, even if it’s my \add own\add* son Jonathan, \add whoever sinned today\add* will certainly die.” But the people didn’t volunteer \add any information\add*.
\v 40 “You all can be on one side, and my son Jonathan and I will be on the other side,” Saul said.
\p “Do whatever feels right to you,” the people replied.
\p
\v 41 Then Saul asked Israel’s god Yahweh, “Show us the truth.” And Saul and Jonathan were selected—not the people.
\v 42 Then Saul asked \add again\add*, “Choose between me and my son Jonathan.” And Jonathan was selected.
\v 43 Saul demanded from Jonathan, “Tell me what you’ve done.”
\p “Actually,” Jonathan answered, “I did taste some honey from the end of my staff, so take me—I’m ready to die.”
\p
\v 44 “May God do to me whatever he wants,” Saul said. “because you will certainly die, Jonathan.”
\p
\v 45 But the people \add stood up to\add* Saul, “Should Jonathan die? Wasn’t it him who saved Israel \add today\add*? We won’t stand for it. As Yahweh lives, not even a hair on his head will be touched because he worked with God today.” And \add so\add* the people rescued Jonathan and he wasn’t executed.
\p
\v 46 Then Saul stopped chasing the Philistines and went home, and the Philistines \add also\add* returned to their place.
\s1 Saul’s kingdom, and his family details
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 47 After Saul had taken on the kingship of Israel, he fought against their enemies from all around: against the Moabites, the Ammonites, the Edomites, the kings of Tsovah, and against the Philistines. Wherever he turned, he punished \add his enemies\add*.
\v 48 He acted bravely and defeated \add even\add* the Amalekites, \add always\add* rescuing Israel from those who \add came in to\add* plunder it.
\p
\v 49 Saul’s sons were Jonathan, Yishvi, and Malkishua. His oldest daughter was Merav, and the younger one was Michal.
\v 50 His wife’s name was Ahinoam (daughter of Ahima’ats). His army commander was Abner (son of Saul’s uncle Ner—
\v 51 Kish was Saul’s father, and Abner’s father Ner was Aviel’s son).
\p
\v 52 The conflict with the Philistines was intensive for all of Saul’s life, so whenever he saw a good warrior or any strong, young man, then he would conscript them.
\c 15
\s1 Saul destroys the Amalekites
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add One day\add* Samuel said to Saul, “It was Yahweh who sent me to anoint you as king over his people Israel \add back then\add*, so now, listen to what he has to say.
\v 2 Commander Yahweh says, ‘I’ve noticed what the Amalekites did to Israel when they came out of Egypt—they attacked them \add as they passed by\add*.
\v 3 So go now and attack Amalek and destroy them completely without compassion, putting men and women, children and infants to death, along with \add their\add* cattle and sheep, camels and donkeys.’ ”
\p
\v 4 So Saul summoned the people to Telaim and counted them: two hundred thousand foot soldiers as well as ten thousand warriors from Judah.
\v 5 Then Saul took them to Amalek city, and they lay in wait in the valley.
\v 6 Then he warned the Keynim, “You all need to move out from among the Amalekites, so that you all won’t be attacked along with them. That’s because you all were kind to our ancestors when they came out of Egypt.” So the Keynim moved out from among the Amalekites.
\p
\v 7 Then Saul slaughtered the Amalekites from Havilah to Shur (near Egypt),
\v 8 and they captured King Agag alive but killed everyone else with swords.
\v 9 However, as well as sparing Agag, they took the best sheep and cattle
\s1 God rejects Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh told Samuel,
\v 11 “I regret that I’ve appointed Saul as king because he’s turned away from following me and hasn’t obeyed my instructions.” That really upset Samuel and he cried out to Yahweh all night.
\v 12 Early the next morning, Samuel got up to meet Saul, but someone told him, “Saul went to Carmel, and wow, he set up a monument to himself. Then he turned around and went to Gilgal.”
\v 13 When Samuel caught up with Saul, Saul told him, “May Yahweh bless you. I’ve followed Yahweh’s instructions.”
\p
\v 14 “Then what’s that bleating of sheep that I can hear?” asked Samuel. “And I’m sure I can hear cattle sounds too?”
\p
\v 15 “\add Ah,\add* the warriors took them from the Amalekites,” Saul answered. “They wanted to save the best of the sheep and the cattle in order to sacrifice to your god Yahweh. But we completely destroyed the rest.”
\p
\v 16 “Stop,” said Samuel, “and let me tell you what Yahweh told me last night.”
\p “Go ahead,” Saul responded.
\p
\v 17 So Samuel told \add him\add*, “Although you consider yourself insignificant, aren’t you the head of the tribes of Israel? Yahweh anointed you as king over Israel,
\v 18 then sent you off with the task of completely destroying the sinful Amalekites—fighting until they were all destroyed.
\v 19 So why didn’t you obey Yahweh’s command? Instead, you headed straight for the plunder and disobeyed Yahweh.”
\p
\v 20 “I did listen to Yahweh’s instructions,” Saul insisted. “and I did what he asked. So now I’ve brought Amalekite King Agag \add here\add* and I’ve completely destroyed the Amalekites.
\v 21 \add Yes,\add* the people kept some of the best sheep and cattle to sacrifice to your god Yahweh here at Gilgal.”
\p
\v 22 But Samuel asked,
\q1 “Is Yahweh happier with burnt offerings and sacrifices
\q1 ^than with those who follow his instructions?
\q1 Listen, obedience is better than sacrifice—
\q1 paying attention is \add worth\add* more than the fat of rams.
\q1
\v 23 Rebellion is as sinful as doing sorcery,
\q1 and being stubborn is as sinful as worshipping idols.
\q1 Because you have rejected Yahweh’s instructions,
\q1 he has rejected you as king.”
\p
\v 24 “\add Yes,\add* I’ve sinned,” Saul responded. “\add I can see that\add* I’ve disobeyed Yahweh’s instruction, and yours too, because I feared the people and did what they were requesting.
\v 25 But now, please forgive my sin and come back with me so I can worship Yahweh.”
\p
\v 26 “\add No,\add* I won’t return with you.” Samuel replied. “You’ve rejected Yahweh’s instructions, and Yahweh has \add already\add* rejected you as Israel’s king.”
\p
\v 27 As Samuel turned around to go, Saul grabbed the edge of his robe and it tore.
\v 28 “Today, Yahweh has torn the Israel kingdom away from you,” Samuel told him. “And he’s given it to another man who’s better than you.
\v 29 And by the way, Israel’s powerful \add God\add* doesn’t go back on his word, and doesn’t change his mind like people tend to do.
\p
\v 30 “\add It’s true that\add* I’ve sinned,” Saul said, “\add but\add* please honour me now in front of the Israelite people and their elders. And then return with me, so I can worship your god Yahweh.”
\v 31 So Samuel followed Saul back, and Saul worshipped Yahweh.
\p
\v 32 Then Samuel commanded, “Bring the Amalekite King Agag over here to me.”
\p So they \add brought\add* him over, and trembling,\f + \fr 15:32 \ft The meaning of the original word here is unclear and so there’ll be different interpretations.\f* he said, “I’ve faced up to death now.”\f + \fr 15:32 \ft Some translations interpret the original words as Agag thinking (or saying to himself) that he was going to be spared, but he surely knew Samuel.\f*
\p
\v 33 Then Samuel told \add him\add*,
\q1 “Just as your sword has made women childless,
\q1 so \add too\add* will your mother be made childless among women.”
\m And Samuel hacked Agag to pieces in front of Yahweh \add there\add* at Gilgal.
\p
\v 34 Then Samuel went \add home\add* to Ramah, and Saul returned to his house in Giveah.
\v 35 \add After that,\add* Samuel never saw Saul again, but he mourned for him, and Yahweh regretted that he’d made Saul king over Israel.
\c 16
\s1 Samuel anoints David as King
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh spoke to Samuel, “How long are you going to keep mourning for Saul for, when I myself have rejected him as king over Israel? Fill your horn with oil and go. I’ll send you to Jesse from Bethlehem, because I’ve seen one of his sons who’d make a \add good\add* king for me.”
\p
\v 2 “How can I do that?” asked Samuel. “Saul would kill me if he heard about it.”
\p “Take a heifer with you,” Yahweh said, “and say, ‘I’ve come to sacrifice to Yahweh.’
\v 3 Invite Jesse to the sacrifice, and I’ll show you what do to from there, and you should anoint the one I tell you to.”
\p
\v 4 So Samuel did what Yahweh had told him, and went to Bethlehem. The town elders trembled when they met him, and asked, “Do you come in peace?”
\p
\v 5 “\add Yes,\add* in peace,” he answered “I’ve come to sacrifice to Yahweh. Purify yourselves and come with me to the sacrifice.” Then he purified Jesse and his sons and called them to the sacrifice.
\p
\v 6 When they arrived, Samuel saw Eliav and thought, “Surely this man’s the one that Yahweh wants to anoint.”
\v 7 But Yahweh told him, “Don’t look at his height or outward appearance, because I’ve rejected him. Because it isn’t what a person sees with their eyes, but Yahweh sees their motives and values.”
\p
\v 8 Then Jesse called for Avinadav to walk across in front of Samuel, but he said again, “Yahweh hasn’t chosen that one.”
\v 9 Then Jesse made Shamah walk across in front, but again he said, “Yahweh hasn’t chosen that one.”
\v 10 So \add all up,\add* Jesse made seven of his sons walk across in front of Samuel, but Samuel told him, “Yahweh hasn’t chosen any of them.”
\v 11 Then Samuel asked Jesse, “Is this all your sons?”
\p “\add Well,\add* there’s just the youngest,” he answered “but you see, he’s \add out\add* looking after the flock.”\f + \fr 16:11 \ft We deliberately left the word ‘flock’ here (rather than ‘sheep’), because although young David is always thought of as ‘the shepherd boy’ in English culture, in the middle-East, \+bd goats\+bd* are probably a more important part of the flock than sheep.\f*
\p “Send for him,” Samuel instructed him, “because we won’t do anything else until he gets here.”
\v 12 So he sent for him and brought him in. He was a good-looker with beautiful eyes and red colouring, and Yahweh said, “Stand up \add and\add* anoint him, because he’s the one.”
\v 13 So Samuel took the horn \add full\add* of \add olive\add* oil and anointed him right there with his brothers, and Yahweh’s spirit rushed onto David from that day onwards. Then Samuel left and went \add home\add* to Ramah.
\s1 Saul employs David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 14 \add Meanwhile,\add* Yahweh’s spirit left Saul, and \add instead\add* an evil spirit from Yahweh tormented him,
\v 15 and his servants told him, “Look, an evil spirit from God is tormenting you.
\v 16 Let our master please get your servants to find a man who knows how to play the harp. Then, whenever the evil spirit from God is on you, then he’ll play it and it’ll be good for you.”
\p
\v 17 “\add Okay,\add*” Saul responded, “Find a man for me who’s good at playing it, and bring him to me.”
\p
\v 18 Then one of his servants spoke up, “Listen, I’ve noticed one of the sons of Jesse in Bethlehem who knows how to play. \add He’s also\add* a very powerful warrior, as well as being good looking and wise in what he says. And Yahweh is with him.”
\p
\v 19 So Saul sent messengers to Jesse to tell him, “Your son David who looks after your flock, send him to me.”
\v 20 So Jesse loaded a donkey with bread, a skin of wine, and a young goat, and sent them to \add King\add* Saul with his son David.
\v 21 When David got to the king, he became his servant, and he was liked so much that he was appointed as the one who carried the king’s equipment.
\v 22 Saul sent \add a message back\add* to Jesse, saying, “Let David remain in my service, because I really like him.”
\v 23 Then whenever the spirit from God tormented Saul, David would take the harp and play it. That would bring relief to Saul and make him feel better, and the evil spirit would leave him.
\c 17
\s1 Goliath Challenges the Israelites
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add At that time,\add* the Philistines gathered their \add army\add* divisions together ready for battle. They assembled at Sokoh in Judah, and camped between Sokoh and Azekah in Efes-Damim.
\v 2 Saul and the Israelite warriors gathered and camped in the Elah valley, then they arranged themselves for battle against the Philistines.
\v 3 So the Philistines stood on one hill and the Israelites on the \add opposite\add* hill, with the valley between them.
\p
\v 4 Then the Philistines sent a champion out from their camp \add to represent them\add*. His name was Goliath and he came from Gath, and he was almost three metres\f + \fr 17:4 \ft Some Greek sources have a height closer to two metres.\f* tall.
\v 5 He wore a bronze helmet, and his body armour had overlapping plates weighing \add a total of\add* some fifty-five kilograms.
\v 6 He had bronze armour on his legs, and a bronze plate\f + \fr 17:6b \ft It’s not totally clear what’s being described in the second part of this sentence, so other interpretations might differ.\f* between his shoulders.
\v 7 The wooden \add shaft of\add* his spear was like a weaver’s beam and his spear time weighed around seven kilograms. His shield-bearer walked in front of him.
\v 8 Goliath stood \add there\add* and called out to the Israelite warriors, “Why have you all come out to line up for battle? \add Now,\add* I’m \add down here as\add* a Philistine, and all of you are Saul’s slaves, \add so\add* choose a man for yourselves and let him come down \add here to fight\add* me.
\v 9 If he’s able to fight with me and kill me, then we’ll become your slaves. But if I’m the winner and kill him, then you’ll all become our slaves and work for us.”
\v 10 Then he said, “I personally scoff at Israel’s lines today. Give me a man so we can fight together.”
\v 11 When Saul and the Israelites heard \add all\add* that, they were discouraged and very afraid.
\s1 David and Goliath
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 12 \f + \fr 17:12 \ft Verses 12–31 aren’t included in every ancient Greek translation.\f*Now David was the son of Jesse from the Efrat clan, he lived in Bethlehem in Judah who had eight sons, and by Saul’s time, was quite old compared to other men.\f + \fr 17:12 \ft There’s a small variation in the original manuscripts at the end of the verse, but it has minimal effect on the essential story-line.\f*
\v 13 Jesse’s three oldest sons (Eliav, Avinadav, and Shamah) had gone to the battle with Saul,
\v 14 but David was the youngest. While the three oldest were with Saul,
\v 15 David went back and forth between Saul’s \add camp\add* and looking after his father’s flock at Bethlehem.
\p
\v 16 For forty days, the Philistine \add champion\add* came out and presented himself in the early morning and \add again\add* in the evening.
\p
\v 17 \add One day\add* Jesse said to his son David, “Here’s a sack of roasted grain and ten loaves of bread. Take them straight away to your brothers at the camp.
\v 18 And take these ten cuts of cheese to the commander of their unit. Find out how your brothers are doing and bring back anything they want to send to me.
\v 19 Saul and them and the other warriors are in the Elah valley, fighting against the Philistines.”
\p
\v 20 So David got up early in the morning and left the flock with a shepherd, and set out just like Jesse had told him to. When he got to the battleground and the camp, the warriors were just going out to the battle line and they were shouting the battle cry.
\v 21 The Israelites and the Philistines arranged \add themselves\add*—battle line to battle line.
\v 22 David left what he’d brought with a guard, and ran into the battle line. Then he caught up with his brothers and asked them how they were doing.
\v 23 While he was talking with them, look, the Philistine champion from Gath was coming out with his challenge. Goliath spoke like he had before and David heard it all.
\v 24 But when the Israelites saw the champion, they were terrified and fled away from him.
\v 25 saying \add to each other\add*, “Have you seen this man who’s coming out? He’s certainly coming to mock Israel! Any man who can defeat him will be made very wealthy by the king, and he’ll give his daughter to him, plus his extended family will be exempt from paying taxes.”
\p
\v 26 “What \add was it that’ll\add* be done for the man who kills this Philistine and take’s Israel’s disgrace away?” David asked some of the men standing around him. “Because who does that uncircumcised Philistine \add think he is\add* that he would taunt the army of the living God?”
\v 27 Then the men told him again what would be given to the man who defeats him.
\p
\v 28 But when David’s oldest brother Eliav heard him talking to the men, he got very angry and scolded him, “Why have you come down here? Who’s looking after those few sheep that you left in the desert? I know you have a big head and you’re just a troublemaker—you \add just\add* want to watch the battle.”
\p
\v 29 “What have I done now?” David asked. “Wasn’t it just a question?”
\v 30 Then he went over to another \add group\add* and asked the same question and got the same answer.
\p
\v 31 So it got around what David was saying, and when Saul heard about it, he sent for him.\f + \fr 17:31 \ft Verses 12–31 aren’t included in every ancient Greek translation.\f*
\v 32 David told the king, “Don’t be discouraged. Your servant will go and fight against this Philistine.”
\p
\v 33 “You can’t go against this Philistine and fight him,” Saul told David. “You’re \add still\add* a lad, but he’s been a professional warrior since he was young.”
\p
\v 34 “Your servant has been \add working\add* for his father tending the flock,” David replied. “\add Sometimes\add* a lion or a bear has come and taken a sheep from the flock
\v 35 and I would go after it and beat it and rescue the sheep from its mouth. If it went to attack me, then I’d grasp it by the jaw and beat it and kill it.
\v 36 Your servant has killed both a lion and a bear, and that uncircumcised Philistine will end up \add just\add* like them, because he’s insulted the army of the living God.”
\v 37 Then he added, “Yahweh who has saved me from the lion and from the bear, \em he\em* will \add be the one to\add* save me from that Philistine.”
\p “Go \add then\add*, and Yahweh be with you.” Saul assented.
\v 38 Then Saul \add had\add* David dressed in his \add own battle\add* attire, and then in body armour with a bronze helmet.
\v 39 David strapped his sword over the top and then tried to walk because he hadn’t been trained \add in wearing it\add*. But he told Saul, “I couldn’t walk in all that without practice,” so he took it \add all\add* off.
\v 40 Then he picked up his staff, and selected five smooth stones from the riverbed. He placed them in the pouch of his shepherd’s bag, and picked up his sling and headed towards the Philistine \add champion\add*.
\s1 David fronts up to Goliath
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 41 Then Goliath came closer and closer to David, with his shield-bearer walking in front of him.
\v 42 When he looked and realised that David was a reddish, good-looking lad, he despised him
\v 43 and asked him, “Are you coming to me with a stick because you think I’m a dog?” Then the Philistine cursed David by his gods.
\v 44 “Come over here,” he told David, “and let me give your flesh to the vultures and wild animals.”
\p
\v 45 “You’re coming to me with a sword and spear and dagger,” David shouted back. “But I’m coming to you in the name of Commander-in-chief Yahweh, the god of Israel’s army that you’ve been taunting.
\v 46 Today, Yahweh will help me defeat you and I’ll knock you down and cut off your head. What’s more, I’ll give the Philistine army corpses to the vultures and wild animals today, then everyone will know that there’s a god in Israel.
\v 47 Everyone here will learn that Yahweh doesn’t save with swords or spears, because he’s in charge of the battle and will help us defeat you all.”
\p
\v 48 Then as Goliath went closer to meet David, David ran quickly towards him at the battle line.
\v 49 He slipped his hand into his bag and took out a single stone and slung it towards the Philistine—striking him on the forehead. The stone sank into his forehead and he collapsed forwards onto the ground,
\v 50 and \add so\add* David defeated the Philistine with a sling and a stone—knocking him down and killing him. Not having a sword with him,
\v 51 David ran and stood over the Philistine—drawing Goliath’s sword out of its sheath to kill him and cut off his head.
\p When the Philistines saw that their powerful champion was dead, they fled
\v 52 and the Israelite warriors started yelling and they chased the Philistines into the valley and as far as Ekron’s gates. The dead bodies of the Philistines \add could be seen\add* on the road all the way from Sha’arayim to Gath and Ekron.
\v 53 When the Israelites returned from chasing the Philistines, they raided the valuables from their camp.
\v 54 David put Goliath’s equipment into his \add own\add* tent, then he carried his head to Jerusalem.
\s1 Saul asks about David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 55 When Saul had seen David going out to meet Goliath, he’d asked Abner, the army commander, “Whose son is this lad, Abner?”
\p “As surely as you live, your majesty,” replied Abner, “I don’t know.”
\p
\v 56 “Find out whose son he is,” the king had commanded.
\p
\v 57 Then when David had returned from killing the Philistine, Abner brought him to stand in front of Saul, and he was holding Goliath’s head.
\v 58 “Whose son are you, lad?” Saul \add asked\add*.
\p “I’m the son of your servant Jesse, who lives in Bethlehem,” replied David.
\c 18
\s1 David’s friendship with Jonathan
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 After David had finished speaking to the king, him and Jonathan became close friends, in fact Jonathan really loved him.
\v 2 \add From\add* that day, Saul kept David \add with him\add*, and wouldn’t allow him to return home.
\v 3 Then Jonathan and David made an agreement together, because Jonathan loved him as much as he loved himself.
\v 4 Jonathan took off the cloak that he was wearing and gave it to David, along with his armour, his sword and bow, and even his belt.
\v 5 David went on missions wherever Saul sent him and \add always\add* succeeded. Saul put him in charge of the warriors, and that pleased all the people, as well as Saul’s servants.
\s1 Saul becomes envious of David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 6 When they’d all come back after killing the Philistine, the women from the Israelite cities had come out to meet King Saul with singing and dancing—playing tambourines and other instruments with great happiness.
\v 7 However, as they danced they sang,
\q1 “Saul has killed his thousands,
\q1 and David his tens of thousands.”
\m
\v 8 Saul found that offensive and it made him very angry. “They’ve attributed tens of thousands to David,” he complained, “but \add only\add* thousands to me. Next thing he’ll end up as king.”
\v 9 So from that day onwards, Saul remained wary of David and kept an eye on him.
\p
\v 10 Then the next day, an evil spirit from God rushed on Saul and \add even\add* in the middle of the house he \add started\add* prophesying. As David was playing near him (as he did every day), Saul was holding a spear
\v 11 and he \add suddenly\add* hurled it, saying, “I’ll pin that David to the wall.” But twice when he did that, David managed to elude him.
\p
\v 12 So Saul \add ended up\add* afraid of David because Yahweh was with him, but was no longer with the king.
\v 13 Then Saul sent him out of the room and appointed him as the commander of a thousand \add warriors\add*, so he came and went in front of the people.\f + \fr 18:13 \ft It’s not entirely clear what the implications of this coming and going were (and similarly at the end of v16). It could easily refer to him returning successfully from military excursions.\f*
\v 14 Yahweh was with David and he was succeeding in everything he did.
\v 15 Saul knew about his successes and couldn’t stand looking at him,
\v 16 but all the people of Israel and Judah loved David because they observed his comings and goings.
\s1 David wins Michal as his wife
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 17 \add One day\add* Saul told David, “Look, \add here’s\add* my oldest daughter Merav—I’ll give her to you for a wife. \add The only condition is that you\add* become a powerful warrior for me and fight Yahweh’s battles.” (Saul had said \add to himself\add*, “I don’t need to hurt him physically—I’ll let the Philistines do that \add for me\add*.”)
\v 18 “Who am I,” David asked, “and who are my relatives in my father’s clan in Israel, that I should become the king’s son-in-law?”
\p
\v 19 However, when it was the time for Saul to give his daughter Merav to David, she was given to Adriel the Meholati to marry.
\p
\v 20 Now Saul’s \add other\add* daughter Michal loved David, and \add when\add* they told the king, he was pleased about it,
\v 21 saying \add to himself\add*, “I’ll give her to him so she can become a trap for him so the Philistines could be the ones to attack him.” So he said a second time, “You’ll become a son-in-law of mine today.”
\v 22 Then Saul commanded his servants, “Tell David privately, ‘Listen, the king is pleased with you, and all of us his servants love you. So now \add we think that you should\add* become the king’s son-in-law.’ ”
\p
\v 23 So they told David privately, but he responded, “Something that significant isn’t likely to happen. I’m hard up and without much honour.”
\p
\v 24 When Saul’s servants told him what David had said,
\v 25 he replied, “Pass this onto David, ‘The king doesn’t want an expensive bride price—only a hundred Philistine foreskins taken from the king’s enemies.’ ” But Saul was \add still wanting\add* the Philistines \add to be the ones\add* to kill David.
\v 26 Now when David heard that from the servants, he decided that he could indeed marry the king’s daughter within the time period that had been given,
\v 27 so he and his men got ready and went and killed \em two\em* hundred Philistine men. He brought them to the king and had them counted to fulfill the pledge, and so Saul \add had to\add* give his daughter Michal to become his wife.
\p
\v 28 But when Saul realised that Yahweh was helping David, and that his daughter loved him,
\v 29 he became more afraid of David, so he remained hostile to David from then on.
\p
\v 30 Whenever the Philistine commanders decided to attack, David was more successful than any other of Saul’s commanders, so he became very famous.
\c 19
\s1 Saul persecutes David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then Saul urged his son Jonathan and all his servants to kill David, but Jonathan really liked David
\v 2 and told him, “My father Saul wants to get rid of you, so please watch out in the morning. Find a hiding place and stay there.
\v 3 And what I’ll do, I’ll go out and stand by my father in the countryside where you’re \add hiding\add*, and I’ll bring you into the conversation to see what he’ll say about you.”
\p
\v 4 So \add the next morning,\add* Jonathan spoke well of David to his father Saul, saying, “Don’t let the king sin against his servant, against David, because he hasn’t sinned against you, and actually, he’s been a big help to you.
\v 5 He took his life into his hands when he fought against Goliath, and Yahweh used it to save all Israel. So why would you sin against innocent blood by putting David to death for no reason.”
\p
\v 6 Saul listened to Jonathan, then he promised, “As surely as Yahweh lives, he won’t be put to death.”
\v 7 \add Afterwards,\add* Jonathan called David and told him what had been said. Then he brought David back \add to work for\add* Saul and things carried on as before.
\p
\v 8 Then war broke out again, and David went out and battled against the Philistines and defeated them so badly that they fled away from him.
\p
\v 9 \add One day\add* as Saul was sitting in his house, an evil spirit from Yahweh came on him. He had his spear in his hand, and David was playing \add the harp\add*.
\v 10 Saul tried to hit David with the spear, but David eluded him and the spear went into the wall. Then David slipped away and fled that night.
\p
\v 11 Saul sent messengers to watch David’s house to kill him in the morning, but his wife Michal told him, “If you don’t \add do something to\add* save your life tonight, you’ll be dead by tomorrow.”
\v 12 So Michal lowered David out through the window, and he was able to slip out and take off.
\v 13 Then she took a household idol and put it in the bed, putting a goats’ hair quilt where the head should be and covering it with the \add bed-\add*clothes.
\v 14 When Saul sent messengers to arrest David, she told them, “He’s sick.”
\v 15 Saul sent the messengers \add back\add*, telling them, “Bring him up to me bed and all so I can kill him.”
\v 16 But when the messengers returned, look, it was a household idol in the bed with a goats’ hair quilt as its head.
\v 17 Saul \add had Michal brought in and\add* questioned, “Why did you deceive me like that and sent my enemy off, so that he slipped away?”
\p “He told me that he’d kill me,” Michal answered Saul, “if I didn’t help him escape.”
\p
\v 18 \add After\add* David had slipped away, he went to Samuel in Ramah and told him everything that Saul had done to him, then the two of them went and stayed in Navit.
\v 19 But Saul was told that David was in Navit near Ramah,
\v 20 so he sent messengers to capture David. When they saw a group of prophets prophesying and Samuel standing as head over them, God’s spirit came onto Saul’s messengers and they also prophesied.
\v 21 They told Saul and he sent more messengers and they also prophesied. He sent messengers a third time, and they \add started\add* prophesying as well.
\v 22 Finally Saul \add himself\add* headed towards Ramah and got as far as the large \add water\add* cistern in Seku. He asked around about Samuel and David, and was told that they were in Navit near Ramah.
\v 23 So he went to Navit, and God’s spirit came on him as well. As he walked, he \add kept\add* prophesying until he entered Navit.
\v 24 He took off his \add outer\add* clothes and prophesied in front of Samuel. He lay undressed all that day and all night, so \add that’s why\add* people ask, “Is Saul a prophet as well?”
\c 20
\s1 Jonathan helps David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then David fled from Navit near Ramah and went to Jonathan and asked, “What have I done? What did I do wrong? What sin has made your father so angry that he wants to take my life?”
\p
\v 2 “Far from it,” Jonathan responded. “You won’t die. Listen, my father doesn’t do anything, big or small, without telling me. So why would my father hide this from me \add if it was his plan\add*? \add No,\add* you’re wrong.”
\p
\v 3 “For sure, your father knows that you’ve taken a liking to me,” said David, “and he would have decided not to tell you so you wouldn’t be upset.” Then David made an oath again, “Indeed, as Yahweh is alive, and as your soul is alive, death is definitely only a step away from me.”
\p
\v 4 “I’ll do anything for you—whatever you say,” Jonathan confirmed.
\p
\v 5 “Listen, the new moon \add celebration\add* is tomorrow, and I’ll definitely be expected to sit with the king to eat,” David told him. “But you \add should\add* let me go, and I’ll hide myself in the countryside until the third evening.
\v 6 If your father misses me, then tell him, ‘David begged me to let him go to his home in Bethlehem, to join his clan for their yearly sacrifice.’
\v 7 If your father says, ‘That’s fine,’ then your servant can relax. But if he gets really angry, you’ll know that he’s planning evil.
\v 8 \add If that’s the case,\add* it’ll be up to you to do what’s right with your servant in terms of our agreement before Yahweh. If I’ve been disloyal to the king, kill me yourself—no need to drag me to him.
\p
\v 9 “Don’t even think of it,” Jonathan responded. “If I find out that my father has decided to harm you, I’ll certainly tell you.”
\p
\v 10 “If your father does have a cruel plan, who’ll tell me?” David asked Jonathan.
\p
\v 11 “Come with me out to the countryside.” Jonathan replied. So they went out to the countryside together
\v 12 and he promised David, “By Yahweh the god of Israel, I will definitely sound out my father around this time tomorrow, or soon after. If it looks good for you, then I’ll certainly send a message to let you know.
\v 13 \add But\add* if he’s planning evil for you, may Yahweh punish me similarly or even worse if I don’t inform you, and send you away so you can leave in peace. And may Yahweh be with you just like he used to be with my father.
\v 14 But as long as I’m still alive, please \add keep\add* our agreement of faithfulness before Yahweh so I won’t die.
\v 15 Don’t ever terminate that agreement of faithfulness towards my family, even when Yahweh destroys your enemies wherever they are.
\v 16 So Jonathan made a multi-generational agreement with David, \add adding\add*, “And may Yahweh punish David’s enemies.”
\p
\v 17 Then Jonathan got David to promise again because he loved him as much as he loved his own life,
\v 18 and he told him, “The new moon \add celebration\add* is tomorrow, and you’ll be missed because your seat will be empty.
\v 19 The following day, go to the place where you hid before and stay by the big rock.
\v 20 I’ll shoot three arrows to the side of it, as if trying to hit a target.
\v 21 Then I’ll send my boy to find the arrows. If I tell him that they’re on my side, then just as Yahweh lives it’ll be safe for you to come \add out\add*.
\v 22 But if I tell the boy that they’re on the far side, go because Yahweh is sending you away.
\v 23 As for our pact, Yahweh will watch over us and our descendants.”
\p
\v 24 So David hid himself in the countryside, and \add at\add* the new moon \add celebrations\add*, the king sat down to eat—
\v 25 sitting at his usual seat by the wall. Jonathan sat opposite, and Abner sat beside Saul. No one was sitting in David’s seat
\v 26 but Saul didn’t say anything that day, thinking, “Something must have happened that made him \add ceremonially\add* unclean.”
\v 27 However, the next day of the celebration when David was still missing, Saul asked his son Jonathan, “Why didn’t Jesse’s son come to the meal yesterday or today?”
\p
\v 28 “David begged me to let him go to Bethlehem,” Jonathan answered.
\v 29 “He requested, ‘Please release me because our clan’s sacrifice will be in the city, and my brother has told me to be there. So now, if I’ve found favour in your eyes, please let me slip away and go to see my brothers.’ That’s why he hasn’t come to the king’s table.”
\p
\v 30 Saul was furious at Jonathan and yelled at him, “\add You\add* son of a perverse, rebellious woman! Do \add you think that\add* I don’t know that you’re taking the side of Jesse’s son? It’s to your detriment and to the shame of your mother’s nakedness,
\v 31 because as long as that son of Jesse stays alive on this earth, you and your kingdom will never be established. Now, send \add men\add* and arrest him, because he’s on his way to the grave!”
\p
\v 32 “Why should he be put to death?” Jonathan retorted. “What \add wrong\add* has he done?”
\p
\v 33 Then Saul hurled his spear at him to kill him, and Jonathan knew \add then\add* that his father had \add already\add* determined to kill David.
\v 34 Jonathan angrily got up from the table. He didn’t eat any food that second day of the celebration—he was upset about David because his father had dishonoured him.\f + \fr 20:34 \ft It’s not entirely clear who this ‘him’ was (as Saul had seriously dishonoured Jonathan as well), so we’ve left it ambiguous (although David was certainly the last name mentioned).\f*
\v 35 So it was that the following morning, Jonathan went out to the countryside at the time agreed with David, taking a small boy \add with him\add*.
\v 36 “Run \add ahead\add*—find the arrows that I’m about to shoot,” he told his boy. The boy ran and he shot the arrow to go over his head.
\v 37 The boy went to where the arrow was, but Jonathan called out, “Isn’t the arrow further past you?”
\v 38 Then he added, “Hurry up! Don’t mess around.” So the boy picked up the arrow and brought it back to his master,
\v 39 but the boy didn’t realise what had just happened—only Jonathan and David knew.
\v 40 Then Jonathan gave his equipment to the boy and sent him back to the city.
\p
\v 41 \add When\add* the boy was gone, David came up from the side and dropped onto his knees, bowing three times with his face to the ground. Then they greeted each other with a kiss and cried together, although David cried the most.
\v 42 Then Jonathan told David, “Go in peace, because us two have made a pact together in Yahweh’s name, agreeing, ‘Yahweh will watch between me and you, and between my descendants and your descendants forever.’ ” Then David set off and Jonathan went back to the city.
\c 21
\s1 David stays at Nov
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 David went to the priest Ahimelek at Nov, but Ahimelek trembled when he saw David and asked him, “Why are you \add here\add* by yourself? How come you don’t have a companion?”
\p
\v 2 “The king sent me on an errand,” David replied, “but he told me not to tell anyone about the matter that he sent me on. Plus I’ve told my young men where to meet me.
\v 3 Now, what food do you have on hand? Give me five loaves of bread or whatever you can find.”
\p
\v 4 “There is no ordinary bread on hand,” the priest answered. “But there is the sacred bread, if only the young men have kept themselves from women.”
\p
\v 5 “There’s definitely been no women near us for three days,” David told the priest. “That’s how I always do missions. The men\f + \fr 21:5 \ft The Hebrew says ‘the men’s vessels’ (or ‘containers’). It’s not culturally clear if that referred to their ‘bodies’ (as this translation implies) or to something they carried.\f* have been pure, even though it was a common journey, and today even more so.”
\p
\v 6 So the priest gave him the sacred bread, because there was no other bread there other than the \add previous day’s\add* bread of the presence which had been removed from in front of Yahweh to be replaced by fresh bread.
\p
\v 7 \add Now it so happened\add* that one of Saul’s servants was there that day presenting himself to Yahweh. He was Doeg the Edomite, a leader of Saul’s herdsmen.
\p
\v 8 David asked Ahimelek, “Do you have any spear or sword here? I didn’t have time to grab my sword or my weapons when I left, because the king’s matter was urgent.”
\p
\v 9 “\add I’ve only got\add* Goliath’s sword,” replied the priest. “\add From\add* the Philistine who you killed in the Elah valley. Look, it’s wrapped in the cloak behind the sacred apron. If you want to take it for yourself, take it, because there’s no other sword around here.”
\p “\add Yes,\add* there’s no other sword like it,” said David. “Get it for me.”
\s1 David flees to Gath
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then David left and \add continued\add* moving to distance himself from Saul, and he went to King Akish of Gath.
\v 11 But Akish’s servants complained to him, “Isn’t that David \add who’ll become\add* king of Israel? Isn’t he the one they sing about when they dance, chanting, ‘Saul has killed his thousands, and David his tens of thousands?’ ”
\p
\v 12 David listened to those words without any reaction because he was very afraid of \add what\add* King Akish \add might do\add*,
\v 13 so he changed his behaviour when he was with them and pretended to be insane. He scratched on \add wooden\add* doors and let his saliva dribble down his beard.
\v 14 Then \add King\add* Akish complained to his servants, “Look, that man’s insane. Why did you all bring him to me?
\v 15 Don’t I already have enough madmen around here—why add another? Must that man come into my house?”
\c 22
\s1 David moves around but gains followers
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 So David left there and slipped away to the cave at Adulam. When his brothers and other relatives found out, they went and \add joined\add* him there.
\v 2 \add Over time,\add* any man who was in trouble, or in debt, or discontented gathered around him, and he became their leader—some four hundred men \add in all\add*.
\p
\v 3 From there, David went to Mitspah in Moab, and he asked the king of Moab, “Please let my parents stay here in the palace with you until I find out what God will do for me.”
\v 4 So he took them to the king where they stayed with him the entire time that David lived in the fortress.
\p
\v 5 Then the prophet Gad told David, “Don’t stay in the fortress. Leave it and come back to Judah’s region.” So David left and went to the Heret forest \add in Judah\add*.
\s1 The slaughter of the priests
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 6 \add One day,\add* Saul was sitting holding his spear underneath the tamarisk tree on a hill \add near\add* Giveah with all his servants stationed around him, when he heard where David and his men were,
\v 7 and he accused his servants, “Listen now, you Benyamites. Will Jesse’s son also\f + \fr 22:7 \ft See note on ‘םַג’ (‘gam’) at https://www.sil.org/resources/publications/entry/99313.\f* give fields and vineyards to all of you? Will he make all of you commanders of thousands and commanders of hundreds?
\v 8 \add Yes,\add* all of you have conspired against me. Not one of you informed me when my \add own\add* son made a pact with \add that\add* son of Jesse. And none of you took my side or even informed me that my son has encouraged my servant to lie in ambush against me as he is today!”
\p
\v 9 Now Doeg the Edomite was stationed there with Saul’s servants, and he \add spoke up\add*, “I saw Jesse’s son go to Nov and talk with Ahimelek \add the priest\add*,
\v 10 and the priest asked Yahweh for directions for him, and gave him food and Goliath’s sword.”
\p
\v 11 Then the king summoned Ahituv’s son Ahimelek and all his relatives who were priests to Nov, and they all came to the king.
\v 12 “Listen now, son of Ahituv,” said Saul.
\p “Go ahead my master,” answered Ahimelek.
\p
\v 13 “Why have you all conspired against me?” asked Saul. “You gave bread to Jesse’s son, and a sword, and you asked guidance from God for him, \add so now\add* he’s risen up against me—lying in ambush this very day?”
\p
\v 14 “Isn’t David one of your most faithful servants?” Ahimelek answered the king. “He’s the king’s son-in-law and commander over your guards, and isn’t he honoured in your house?
\v 15 Did I begin to inquire for him from God today? Far be it from me! Don’t let the king accuse his servant or any of my father’s household, because your servant doesn’t know about any of this—nothing at all.”
\p
\v 16 “For certain, Ahimelek,” the king declared. “you and all your father’s household will certainly die \add for that\add*!”
\v 17 Then the king commanded his messengers who were stationed beside him, “Turn around and put Yahweh’s priests to death because they’ve taken David’s side. And because they knew he was fleeing, but they didn’t inform me.” But the king’s servants weren’t willing to attack Yahweh’s priests.
\v 18 So the king commanded Doeg the Edomite, “You turn around and attack the priests.” So Doeg turned and he himself attacked the priests. He killed eighty-five men that day who wore sacred linen aprons.
\v 19 Also \add at\add* Nov (the city of priests) he killed men and women with his sword, children and infants, cattle, donkeys, and sheep.
\p
\v 20 But one of Ahimelek’s sons was able to slip away. His name was Abiathar and he fled \add to join\add* David,
\v 21 and informed him that Saul had \add commanded Doeg to\add* kill Yahweh’s priests.
\v 22 “I knew on that day,” David responded, “when Doeg the Edomite was there, that he would probably tell Saul. It’s my fault that your father and all his family were murdered.
\v 23 Stick with me—don’t be afraid, because the one who wants to kill me would kill you as well. You’ll be safe with me.”
\c 23
\s1 David rescues Ke’ilah
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Some time later,\add* David was told, “Listen, the Philistines are fighting against Ke’ilah \add city\add* and they are stealing grain from the threshing floors,”
\v 2 so he asked Yahweh, “Should I go and attack those Philistines?”
\p “Go and attack the Philistines and save Ke’ilah,” Yahweh responded.
\p
\v 3 But David’s men warned him, “Listen, we’re afraid here in Judah, and we’d be even more afraid if we went to Ke’ilah against the Philistines’ \add battle\add* lines.”
\v 4 So David double-checked with Yahweh who answered, “Get ready \add and\add* go to Ke’ilah, because I’m about to give you victory over the Philistines.”
\v 5 Then David and his men went to Ke’ilah and battled against the Philistines—thoroughly defeating them and taking their livestock, thus saving the inhabitants of Ke’ilah.
\p
\v 6 Now when Ahimelek’s son Abiathar had fled to David at Ke’ilah, he’d brought a sacred apron with him.
\p
\v 7 Saul was told that David had gone to Ke’ilah, so he said, “\add Ah,\add* God has deserted him and given him to me because he’s shut himself in by going into a \add walled\add* city with doors that can be barred shut.”
\v 8 So Saul initiated preparations to go to Ke’ilah to entrap David and his men.
\p
\v 9 But David discovered that Saul was planning to destroy him, so he summoned Abiathar the priest, “Bring the sacred apron here.”
\v 10 Then David prayed, “Yahweh, God of Israel, your servant has heard that Saul is intending to come to Ke’ilah—to destroy the city because of me.
\v 11 Will Saul come here, just as your servant has heard? Will the masters of Ke’ilah turn me over to him? Yahweh, God of Israel, please tell your servant.”
\p “\add Yes,\add* he will go there,” Yahweh answered.
\p
\v 12 “\add And\add* will the masters of Ke’ilah hand me and my men over to Saul?” David asked \add again\add*.
\p And Yahweh said, “They will deliver up.”
\p
\v 13 So David and his men (about 600 of them), left Ke’ilah and moved around from place to place. When Saul was told that David had slipped away from Ke’ilah, he didn’t continue with his plans.
\s1 Saul pursues David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 14 David stayed in \add various\add* fortresses in the wilderness, and \add sometimes\add* he stayed in the hill country in the Zif wilderness. Saul kept searching for them, but God didn’t allow him to capture them.
\v 15 \add Once\add* David was \add staying\add* in the Zif wilderness and he saw that Saul had arrived there to try to kill him.
\v 16 Then Saul’s son Jonathan went to David at Horesh and encouraged him to keep trusting God,
\v 17 saying, “Don’t be afraid, because my father Saul won’t capture you. As for you, you’ll reign over Israel, and I’ll be your second in command. Even my father knows that.”
\v 18 \add Again,\add* the two of them made an agreement before Yahweh. Then David stayed at Horesh and Jonathan returned home.
\p
\v 19 \add Some men\add* from Zif went to Saul at Giveah, saying, “We’re pretty sure that David’s hiding himself among us in the fortresses in Horesh, on the Hakilah hill south of Yeshimon.
\v 20 So now, your majesty, come over whenever you want, and we’ll make sure he’s handed over to you.”
\p
\v 21 “May you all be blessed by Yahweh,” Saul responded, “because you’ve had compassion on me.
\v 22 Please go back and double-check and find out who’s seen him there. Learn more and look at the place yourselves, because I’ve been told that he’s very crafty.
\v 23 Find out all the other places where he hides himself. Return to me with definite \add information\add*, then I’ll go with you all, and if he’s there in that region, then I’ll search for him among the population of Judah.”
\p
\v 24 So they left and returned to Zif ahead of Saul. \add At that time,\add* David and his men were in the Maeon wilderness south of Yeshimon
\v 25 when Saul and his men came to search \add for them\add*. When David was told, he went \add further south\add* to a rocky hill therein that wilderness, but Saul \add also\add* heard \add that\add* and followed them.
\v 26 \add At one point,\add* Saul was on one side of a hill, and David and his men were on the other side. David was hurrying to get away from Saul, but Saul and his men were trying to surround David and his men to capture them.
\v 27 \add Just\add* then, a messenger arrived for Saul, saying, “Come quickly because the Philistines have raided our region.”
\v 28 So Saul had to give up chasing David and went to repel the Philistines.
\v 29 David \add also\add* left that area and stayed in the fortresses around Eyn-Gedi.
\c 24
\s1 David refuses to kill Saul
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 After Saul returned from repelling the Philistines, they told him that David was \add now\add* in the Eyn-Gedi wilderness.
\v 2 So Saul chose three thousand Israelite warriors and went searching for David and his men at Wild Goats Rocks.
\v 3 On the way, as they reached the sheep yards, Saul went into a cave to relieve himself, but David and his men were hiding there further inside the cave.
\v 4 David’s men whispered to him, “Wow, today what Yahweh told you is coming true \add when he said\add*, ‘Listen, I’m about to give victory over your enemy, and you will do to him whatever you think is right.’ ” Then David sneaked up and quietly cut \add a piece\add* off the edge of Saul’s robe.
\v 5 Afterwards \add however,\add* David felt bad because he’d cut \add a piece\add* off Saul’s clothes
\v 6 and he said to his men, “By Yahweh, I shouldn’t have done that to my master—to Yahweh’s anointed \add king\add*—to do him harm because he’s the \add one\add* Yahweh anointed.”
\v 7 He restrained his men with \add a few quiet\add* words and wouldn’t allow them to attack Saul.
\p Then Saul left the cave and headed down the path.
\v 8 After waiting a few moment, David went out of the cave and called out behind Saul, “My master the king.” When Saul turned and looked behind him, David bowed
\v 9 and said, “Why do you listen to people when they say that I’m wanting to harm you?
\v 10 Look, you can see for yourself that Yahweh gave me the opportunity to harm you today in the cave, and some \add of my men\add* wanted me to kill you, but I showed you compassion and told them that I wouldn’t do harm to my master, because he’s Yahweh’s chosen king.
\v 11 Your majesty, look of this piece of your robe that I’m holding, because when I cut it off, I didn’t kill you. So now you can see for yourself that I have no evil plans, and I haven’t do you any wrong, even though you’re ambushing me so you can kill me.
\v 12 May Yahweh judge between you and me because I’ll let Yahweh repay you for any harm against me—it won’t be me who works against you.
\v 13 As the ancient proverb says, ‘Evil things are done by evil people.’ But it won’t be me who harms you.
\v 14 Who has Israel’s king come to find? Who are you chasing after? A dead dog? A flea?
\v 15 May Yahweh take his judgement seat and judge between me and you, and may he see my case and find me innocent and save me from you.”
\p
\v 16 When David finished saying that to Saul, Saul called back, “Is that your voice, my son David?” Then he cried loudly
\v 17 and told David, “You’re more righteous than I \add am\add*, because you were good to me even when I did evil \add things\add* to you.
\v 18 You’ve stated that you’ve treated me well—how Yahweh gave you the advantage over me, yet you didn’t kill me.
\v 19 Now if a man finds his enemy \add unprepared\add*, does he \add just\add* send him off happily? Well, may Yahweh reward you for your mercy towards me today.
\v 20 Now listen, I know for certain that you’re going to become king and that the Israel kingdom will prosper under your leadership.
\v 21 Now promise me by Yahweh that you won’t destroy my name from my family line by killing my descendants.”
\v 22 David promised that to Saul, then Saul went back home and David and his men returned to their fortress.
\c 25
\s1 David, Naval, and Abigil
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Later on,\add* Samuel died and all the Israelites gathered and mourned for him, then they buried him at his house in Ramah.
\p Then David left and went to the Paran wilderness.
\v 2 Now there was a man in Maeon whose business was in Carmel. He owned three thousand sheep and one thousand goats, and was \add busy\add* shearing his sheep at Carmel.
\v 3 The man’s name was Naval, and his wife was Abigail. The wife was wise and very good-looking, whereas the man (who was a Kalevi) was harsh and cruel.
\p
\v 4 While David was in the wilderness, he heard that Naval was shearing his sheep,
\v 5 so he told ten young men, “Go to Naval at Carmel and greet him in my name.
\v 6 Then tell him, ‘Make you live long. Peace to you and your household and everything that belongs to you.
\v 7 Now I’ve heard those shearers are working for you, and your shepherds have been living with us. We haven’t harmed them or taken anything of theirs while they’ve been in Carmel.
\v 8 Ask your servants and they’ll confirm that. May \add these\add* young men enjoy your favour because \add harvesting the wool\add* is a prosperous time, so I request a gift for your servants and for your ‘son’, David.’ ”
\p
\v 9 So David’s men went and passed all that onto Naval, and then waited for his response.
\v 10 But Naval answered them, “Why should I care about David? Jesse’s son isn’t anyone important. There’s lot of servants these days who are on the run from their masters.
\v 11 This bread and water, and the meat that I’ve butchered, is for my shearers. Why should I give it to men when I don’t even know where they’re from?”
\p
\v 12 David’s men left and returned to inform him what \add Naval\add* had been said.
\v 13 Then David told his men, “Strap on your swords.” So David took around four hundred men, leaving around two hundred with their gear.
\p
\v 14 \add Meanwhile\add*, Naval’s wife Abigail had been informed by a servant, “Listen, David sent messengers from the wilderness to greet our master, but he yelled at them.
\v 15 Their men had been very good to us—we were never harmed or had anything go missing when we were in the countryside.
\v 16 They protected us both day and night for the whole time that we were tending the sheep.
\v 17 So now you should think about it and decide what you can do, because \add no doubt\add* disaster is about to hit our master and all his household. He’s a terrible man who listens to no one.”
\p
\v 18 So Abigail quickly \add collected\add* two hundred loaves of bread, two skins of wine, five butchered sheep, a large basket of roasted grain, a hundred bunches of raisins, and two hundred cakes of figs. She loaded \add it all\add* on donkeys
\v 19 and told her servants, “Go ahead \add with the donkeys\add*. I’ll come \add along\add* behind you all.” But she didn’t say a word to her husband Naval.
\p
\v 20 As she rode down a hill and into the ravine, suddenly she encountered David and his men coming down to meet her.
\v 21 David had told them, “\add Well\add* it was certainly a waste of time looking after that fellow in the wilderness, and taking such care not to take anything of his. Then all he did was to return insults for our good behaviour.
\v 22 May God \add preserve\add* my enemies, if I don’t eliminate all of the older and young men in his household before the morning.
\p
\v 23 When Abigail saw David, she quickly dismounted from the donkey and fell to her knees in front of David and bowed \add her face\add* to the ground.
\v 24 She clutched his feet and said, “My master, let the guilt be on me myself. Please let your female servant explain to you—please listen to what your female servant has to say.
\v 25 My master, please don’t remain angry at that worthless man, Naval. His name means ‘fool’ and so he is. He’s a senseless man, but my master, your female servant didn’t see the young men you sent.
\v 26 And now, my master, as Yahweh lives and as your spirit lives, it’s Yahweh who’s prevented you from spilling blood and taking his punishment into your own hands. And now may your enemies and anyone who makes plans against my master be like Naval.
\v 27 Now let this gift that your servant has brought to my master be given to the young men who serve my master.
\v 28 Please forgive your female servant for being forward because Yahweh will certain advance my master’s reputation. My master is fighting Yahweh’s battles and all your life, you haven’t broken the law.
\v 29 Even if someone decides to chase you and try to kill you my master, your god Yahweh will keep you wrapped in his bundle of the living, whereas your enemies will be slung away like stones from a sling.
\v 30 If Yahweh does all the good things he’s promised for my master, then \add in time\add* you’ll become Israel’s ruler.
\v 31 It would be good not to have someone’s unnecessary death as an obstacle for you or to be seen as my master \add just\add* saving his own skin. Then Yahweh will bless my master, and you will appreciate your female servant.”
\p
\v 32 “May Israel’s god Yahweh be blessed,” David responded. “\add The one\add* who sent you to meet me today.
\v 33 And blessed be your discernment and blessed be you \add yourself\add*, because you’ve prevented me from shedding blood today and from taking my own revenge.
\v 34 \add Yes\add* indeed, as Yahweh lives, Israel’s god prevented me from harming you. If you hadn’t hurried here to meet me, certainly neither Naval nor any of his older or young men would have survived \add to see\add* the morning light.
\v 35 Then David accepted the gifts that she had brought for him, and said, “Go back to your house in peace. See, I’ve listened to what you said and granted your request.”
\p
\v 36 When Abigail got home to Naval, wow, he had put on a celebration at his house with a feast fit for a king. He was feeling very contented and was quite drunk, so she didn’t tell him anything at all until it was light in the morning.
\v 37 And so it was in the morning, when Naval’s wine had worn off, his wife told him what she’d done. \add In his rage,\add* he had a stroke and became totally paralysed
\v 38 for about then days. Then Yahweh struck Naval and he died.
\p
\v 39 When David heard that Naval had died, he said, “Blessed be Yahweh, who has judged the case of Naval’s insult and has prevented his servant from doing the wrong thing. And Yahweh has repaid Naval for his own evil.”
\p Then David sent \add messengers\add* to Abigail about taking her as a wife.
\v 40 His servants went to her at Carmel and told her, “David sent us to take you \add back\add* to become his wife.”
\p
\v 41 She knelt down with her face to the ground and replied, “See, your female servant is \add ready\add* for washing the feet of my master’s servants like a slave woman.”
\v 42 Then Abigail and five of her servant girls quickly got ready, and she mounted the donkey. She followed the messengers to where David was and became a wife for him.
\p
\v 43 David had also married Ahinoam from Jezreel, so both of them became his wives.
\v 44 (Meanwhile Saul had given his daughter, David’ wife Michal, to Palti, Layish’s son from Galim.)
\c 26
\s1 David spares Saul again
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add One time some\add* Zifim went to Saul at Giveah and told him, “Isn’t David hiding himself on the Hakilah hill opposite Yeshimon?”
\v 2 So Saul got ready with three thousand chosen Israelite warriors and went to the Zif wilderness to search for David.
\v 3 They camped at Hakilah hill (opposite Yeshimon) by the path, but David stayed in the wilderness when he saw the Saul was there trying to get him.
\v 4 (He’d sent some spies and knew that Saul had definitely arrived.)
\v 5 \add That night,\add* David moved out and got to the place where Saul had camped. He could see where Saul was sleeping, along with Ner’s son Abner, his army commander. Saul was asleep in the secure centre with his warriors all around him.
\p
\v 6 David spoke to Ahimelek (the Hittite) and to Avishay (son of Joab’s brother Tseruyah) asking, “Who’ll go into the camp with me to Saul?”
\p Avishay answered, “I will. I’ll go with you.”
\p
\v 7 So that night David and Avishay crept into the camp. Saul was asleep there in the centre with his spear stuck into the ground near his head, and Abner and others were lying \add in a circle\add* around him.
\v 8 Avishay whispered to David, “Today God has arranged for you to defeat your enemy. So please let me pierce him now with the spear through him and into the ground. Only one strike will do it—I won’t have to strike him a second time.
\p
\v 9 “Don’t kill him,” David responded, “because who can attack Yahweh’s anointed king and be considered innocent?”
\v 10 As Yahweh lives, he will certainly strike him, or \add else\add* his time will come and he’ll die, or \add maybe\add* he’ll go into battle and be swept away.
\v 11 May Yahweh prevent me from harming Yahweh’s anointed one. Just grab the spear that’s by his head, and the jug of water, then we can get out of here.”
\v 12 So David took the spear and water jug that were near Saul’s head and then got out of there. No one saw them, and no one woke up or realised, because Yahweh had caused them all to fall into a deep sleep.
\p
\v 13 Then David crossed the valley and stood on the top of the \add opposite\add* hill—quite a distance away
\v 14 and called out to Abner and the camp, “Won’t you answer, Abner?”
\p “Who are you that’s shouting to the king?” Abner answered.
\p
\v 15 “Aren’t you a warrior?” David called out. “And who in Israel is as good as you? But why didn’t you watch over your master the king? Because one of the people came to destroy the king your master.
\v 16 Your performance wasn’t good. As Yahweh lives, all of you who were supposed to be watching over your master, over Yahweh’s anointed one, deserve to be executed. Look around, where’s the king’s spear and water jug that were by his head?”
\p
\v 17 Then Saul recognised David’s voice and called back, “Is that your voice, my son David?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* it is my voice, my master the king.” David replied.
\v 18 Then he asked, “Why is my master chasing his servant? What have I done? What wrong am I guilty of?
\v 19 Now please listen, my master the king, to your servant’s words. If it’s Yahweh who’s incited you against me, may he accept an offering, but if it’s people, may they be cursed in front of Yahweh because they’ve driven me away from enjoying Yahweh’s inheritance for me,\f + \fr 26:19 \ft This is possibly referring to David not being able to enjoy his piece of land, but we haven’t said that in the translation because it’s not certain. (It could be referring to having a peaceful or prosperous life, for example.)\f* saying, ‘Go \add away and\add* serve other gods.’
\v 20 So now, don’t let me die far from Yahweh’s presence. \add It seems that\add* Israel’s king has come \add all this way\add* to chase a flea, just like someone chases a wild bird in the hills.”
\p
\v 21 Saul responded, “\add Yes,\add* I’ve sinned. Go home, my son David. I won’t wrong you again, because you spared my life today. Yes, I’ve acted foolishly and been following the wrong path.”
\p
\v 22 “Look, here’s your spear.” David called back. “Get one of your young men to come across and get it.
\v 23 May Yahweh repay each person for their obedience and faithfulness. Yahweh put you in a vulnerable position today, but I wasn’t willing to hurt Yahweh’s anointed \add king\add*.
\v 24 Just as I honoured your life today, so \add to\add* may Yahweh honour my life and rescue me from all my difficulties.
\p
\v 25 “May you be blessed, my son David,” Saul told David. “You certainly have both ability and the skill to use it.”
\p Then David went on his way and Saul went back home.
\c 27
\s1 David among the Philistines
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 But David thought to himself, “One of these days, I’m going to be caught off-guard by Saul’s actions. There’s no future for me \add here\add*, so I definitely \add need to\add* slip away to the Philistines region. Then \add hopefully\add* Saul will give up looking for me again across all Israel, and I’ll slip be able to stay under his radar.”
\v 2 So he left \add Israel\add* and crossed over with his six hundred men to Gath \add and\add* King Akish (Maeok’s son).
\v 3 Then David and his men settled with Akish in Gath. David had his two wives Ahinoam (from Jezreel and Abigail (widow of Naval from Carmel) with him.
\v 4 When Saul was told that David had fled to Gath, he stopped searching for him.
\p
\v 5 \add One day,\add* David asked Akish, “If I’ve found favour in your eyes, ask them to give me a place in one of the cities in the countryside so I can live there. There’s no need for us to bludge on you here in the royal city.”
\v 6 So Akish gave Tsiklag to David that day, \add and\add* as a result, it still belongs to Judah’s kings until today.
\p
\v 7 David \add ended up\add* living in the Philistine countryside for \add around\add* sixteen months,
\v 8 and \add during that time\add*, he would take his men to raid the Geshurim, the Gizrim, and the Amalekites. They had lived in that region from long before—from Shur all the way to Egypt.
\v 9 When David attacked a place, he would take the sheep and cattle, donkeys and camels, and clothes, but he didn’t leave anyone alive. Then he’d return home and visit Akish.
\v 10 The king would ask him, “Which place did you raid today?”
\p David would tell him, “In southern Judah,” or “South of the Yerahme’elim,” or “In the southern Keyni \add region\add*”.
\v 11 David never allowed any man or woman to live to be able to go back to Gath and report on what he’d \add really\add* been doing. That was how he handled things for the entire time that he lived in the Philistine countryside.
\v 12 But \add King\add* Akish was trusting David, thinking, “He’ll definitely be hated \add now by\add* his people in Israel, so he’ll be forced to remain here as my servant.”
\c 28
\s1 Saul consults a medium
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Some time later, the Philistines gathered their forces \add getting ready\add* to battle against Israel, and \add King\add* Akish complimented David, “We’ll definitely want you and your men to join with our forces.”
\p
\v 2 “\add Sounds good\add*,” David replied, “because you’ll be able to see for yourself what your servant can do.”
\p “I’ll appoint as my permanent bodyguard,” Akish told him.
\p
\v 3 By now, Samuel was long dead and all Israel had mourned for him and had buried him in his town of Ramah, and Saul had had expelled all the mediums from the country, along with those who contacted the spirits.
\p
\v 4 Then the Philistines went to Shunem and camped \add there\add*. Meanwhile Saul gathered all Israel’s \add warriors\add* and they camped at Gilboa,
\v 5 but when he saw the Philistine camp, he was afraid and trembled inside.
\v 6 He asked Yahweh \add for direction\add*, but Yahweh didn’t answer him either through a dream, nor by \add use of\add* the sacred pouch, nor through any prophet.
\v 7 Then Saul instructed his servants, “Find a woman for me who talks to the spirits of dead people, so I can go to her and ask her something.”
\p “Sure,” his servants told him, “there’s a woman in Eyn-Dor who can do that.”
\p
\v 8 So Saul put on different clothes to disguise himself, and went off in the night with two of his men. They went to the woman and he asked, “Please divine for me with a spirit of the dead and bring up the person that I tell you.”
\p
\v 9 “Listen,” said the woman, “surely you’re aware of what Saul has done—he’s expelled anyone out of the country brings back the dead or converses with the spirits. So why are you trying to trap me? Are you trying to get me killed?”
\p
\v 10 Saul promised her in front of Yahweh, “As Yahweh lives, you won’t be punished for doing this.”
\p
\v 11 “\add Okay\add* then, who \add do you want me to\add* bring up for you?”
\p “Bring Samuel up for me.” he replied.
\p
\v 12 When the woman saw Samuel, she yelled out and said to Saul, “\add Oh no,\add* you’ve tricked me! You’re Saul!”
\p
\v 13 “Don’t be afraid,” said the king, “but what have you seen?”
\p “I saw gods coming up out of the ground,” she replied.
\p
\v 14 “What does he look like?” he asked.
\p “An old man’s appearing,” she said, “wearing a robe.”
\p Saul knew it was Samuel, and he knelt down and bowed his face to the ground.
\p
\v 15 “Why’ve you disturbed me by bringing me up?” Samuel asked.
\p “I’m very distressed,” Saul replied, “and the Philistines are about to go to war against me. And God has turned away from me and doesn’t answer me anymore—he hasn’t sent me any prophets or given me any dreams, So I’ve called to you so you can tell me what I should do.”
\p
\v 16 “If Yahweh’s turned away from you and become your enemy,” Samuel responded, “what’s the point in asking me?”
\v 17 He’s \add simply\add* done what he told me to tell you that he’d do. He’s ripped the kingdom away from you and given it to your neighbour David.
\v 18 You didn’t do what Yahweh instructed and you didn’t execute his anger against Amalek, so that’s why Yahweh is doing that to you today.
\v 19 He will also allow Israel to be defeated by the Philistines, and tomorrow you and your sons will be \add here\add* with me. Yahweh will also allow Israel’s \add entire\add* camp \add of warriors\add* to be captured by the Philistines.”
\p
\v 20 Saul, who’d been standing immediately toppled right over and crashed to the ground—he was very scared by Samuel’s words, plus he didn’t have much strength because he hadn’t eaten for twenty-four hours.
\v 21 The woman knelt over Saul and saw that he was terrified, and told him, “Listen to me. Your female servant has risked my life to do what you said and follow your instructions.
\v 22 So now, although you’re the king, please listen to your female servant. Let me get you some food, and then eat it to regain your strength for the journey \add home\add*.
\p
\v 23 But he refused, saying, “\add No,\add* I won’t eat anything.” However his servants and also the woman urged him, and he decided to take their advice so he got up from the ground and sat on the bed.
\v 24 Now the woman had a calf in a stall at the house, and she quickly slaughtered it, She took flour and kneaded it and baked flat bread.
\v 25 She placed the food in front of Saul and his servants, and they ate before getting up and going out into the night.
\c 29
\s1 The Philistines don’t trust David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile,\add* the Philistines had gathered \add in\add* their camp at Afek, while the Israelites were camped near the Jezreel spring.
\v 2 As the Philistine leaders marched out groups of hundreds and thousands, David and his men were behind them with \add King\add* Akish.
\v 3 But the Philistine commanders asked, “What are those Hebrews \add doing here\add*?”
\p “That’s David,” Akish replied. “He was the servant of King Saul of Israel, but he’s been with me for over a year, and I haven’t found anything \add distrustful\add* in him from when he lost favour until today?”
\p
\v 4 But the Philistine commanders were furious at him and told him, “Send that man back to whatever place you’ve given him! There’s no way he’s joining us in the battle! What if he turned against us during the fight? What better way could he have to win back his master’s favour than by killing our men?
\v 5 Isn’t this the David that when they dance they sing,
\q1 ‘Saul has struck down his thousands,
\q1 and David his ten thousands’?”
\p
\v 6 So Akish called David and told him, “As Yahweh lives, you’ve definitely been honest, and everything I’ve seen of your activities has been good, because I haven’t seen you do anything wrong since the day you first arrived. However, these rulers don’t approve of you,
\v 7 so turn back and go in peace so that you don’t do anything to displease them.”
\p
\v 8 “But what have I done?” David rebutted. “What \add wrong\add* have you found in your servant from the day I arrived at your place until now? Why can’t I come and fight against the enemies of my master the king?”
\p
\v 9 “All I know, is that as far as I’m concerned you’re as reliable as a messenger from God.” Akish responded. “Nevertheless, the Philistine commanders have said, ‘He can’t join our side in the battle.’
\v 10 So you and your master’s servants who came with you should get up with the early morning light and leave.”
\p
\v 11 So David and his men packed up early in the morning to return to the Philistines region, but the Philistine \add warriors\add* headed towards Jezreel.
\c 30
\s1 David pursues the Amalekites
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 David and his men got back to Tsiklag three days later and \add found that\add* the Amalekites had raided Tsiklag and other parts of the wilderness in southern Judah. They had attacked Tsiklag then burnt it down,
\v 2 capturing the women and children and everyone else. They hadn’t killed anyone, but had taken them away \add with them\add*,
\v 3 so when David and his men arrived at the town, wow, it had been burnt down and their wives, and sons and daughters had been taken captive.
\v 4 David and his men cried loudly until they didn’t have the strength to cry any more.
\v 5 David’s two wives, Ahinoam (from Jezreel and Abigail (Naval’s widow from Carmel), had been taken captive \add along with the others\add*.
\p
\v 6 David was in a tight spot because the men \add were considering\add* throwing rocks at him to kill him, because they were very upset about their children, but he found strength in his god Yahweh.
\v 7 Then David asked the priest Abiathar (Ahimelek’s son), “Please get the sacred apron and come back to me.” So Abiathar got the sacred apron and went back to David.
\v 8 Then David asked Yahweh, “If I chase after these raiders, will I catch up to them?”
\p “Chase them because you’ll definitely catch up and you’ll certainly be able to rescue the captives,” Yahweh answered.
\p
\v 9 So David and the six hundred men who were with him took off and they reached the Besor riverbed, where some of the slower ones remained.
\v 10 David and four hundred men continued, but two hundred more of the men who were exhausted stopped at \add the far end of\add* the Besor riverbed.
\v 11 They found an Egyptian man in the countryside and took him to David. They gave him water to drink and bread to eat,
\v 12 along with a slice of fig cake and two raisin clusters. He ate it and felt stronger again because he hadn’t eaten or drunk for three full days.
\v 13 “Who do you belong to?” David asked. “And where are you from?”
\p “I am an Egyptian,” he replied. “I’m an Amalekite man’s slave but my master left me three days ago because I got sick.
\v 14 We’d raided the southern area of the Kereti as well as part of Judah and the south of Caleb. And we burnt down Tsiklag.”
\p
\v 15 David asked him, “Can you lead us to them?”
\p “I’ll take you to them,” he answered, “\add if you\add* promise by God that you won’t kill me and won’t hand me back over to my master.”
\v 16 So he took David down \add to the plain\add*, and sure enough, there were the Amalekites spread out, eating and drinking and celebrating because of all the plunder that they had taken from the Philistine and Judah regions.
\v 17 David attacked them from twilight until the evening of the next day, and none of the Amalekites were able to escape except for four hundred young men who jumped on camels and fled.
\v 18 So David was \add able to\add* rescue everyone that the Amalekites had taken, including his two wives.
\v 19 None of their sons or daughters were missing, and they \add also recovered\add* \add all the animals and\add* all the plunder—nothing was missing—big or small.
\v 20 David took all the flocks and herds and they drove them in front of the other livestock, saying, “This is David’s spoil.”
\p
\v 21 Then David got back to the two hundred men who’d been too exhausted to go all the way with them. They’d stayed at the Besor riverbed, and they went out to meet David and his men. As David approached to greet them,
\v 22 all the evil and worthless men who’d gone with David complained, “Because they didn’t go with us, we won’t give them any of the plunder that we recovered, except that each man can get his wife and children then take them away and leave.”
\p
\v 23 “\add No\add*, my brothers,” David said. “You won’t do that with what Yahweh has given to us. He’s protected us and helped us defeat the raiders who came against us.
\v 24 Who’ll listen to you all when you talk like that? The portion for the ones who went and fought will be the same as for those who stayed with the equipment. They’ll all share alike.
\v 25 So from that time onwards, it’s been a custom and law in Israel.
\s1 David distributes gifts
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 26 When David got \add back\add* to Tsiklag, he sent \add a gift from\add* some of the plunder to the elders in Judah, telling them, “Look, here’s a blessing for you—some of the spoil from Yahweh’s enemies.”
\v 27 \add Gifts were sent to:\add* those in Bethel, Ramot-Negev, Yatir,
\v 28 Aroer, Sifmot, Eshtemoa,
\v 29 Rakal, the cities of the Yerahme’elim and the Keynim,
\v 30 Hormah, Bor-Ashan, Atak,
\v 31 Hebron, and all the places where David and his men had been to.
\c 31
\s1 The death of Saul and his sons
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Chr. 10:1-12)
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile,\add* the Philistines were battling against Israel, and the Israelites fled from the Philistines but \add many\add* were killed on Mt. Gilboa.
\v 2 The Philistines stayed hard on the tail of Saul and his sons, and they killed his sons Jonathan, Avinadav, and Malkishua.
\v 3 Then the battle was fierce around Saul, then the Philistine archers sighted him and he writhed in terrible pain from the arrows.
\v 4 He said to the man carrying his equipment, “Draw out your sword and pierce me through with it, lest these uncircumcised Philistines come and pierce me through then torture me as I’m dying.” But the man who carried his equipment was very scared and wouldn’t do it, so Saul took his sword and fell on it.
\v 5 When the man carrying his equipment saw that the king was dead, he too fell on his sword and died with him.
\v 6 So Saul, three of his sons, and the man carrying his equipment all died on that same day, along with all his warriors.
\v 7 When the Israelites on the \add north\add* side of the \add Jezreel\add* valley and on the east side of the Jordan saw that the Israelite warriors had fled and that Saul and his sons had been killed, they left their cities and fled, and the Philistines came and lived in them.
\p
\v 8 The next day when the Philistines came to strip \add anything valuable from\add* the bodies, they found the bodies of Saul and his three sons \add there\add* on Mt. Gilboa.
\v 9 They cut off the king’s head and took all his gear. Then they sent \add messengers\add* all around the Philistine region to spread their good news to the temples of their idols and to \add all\add* their people.
\v 10 They Saul’s armour and weapons in the temple of the Ashtarot, and hung his body on the Beyt-Shean \add city\add* wall.
\p
\v 11 When the residents of Yavesh-Gilead heard what the Philistines had done to Saul’s \add body\add*,
\v 12 all their warriors walked all night to take Saul’s body and those of his sons, off the wall at Beyt-Shean. They \add took them back\add* to Yavesh and burnt them there.
\v 13 Then they took their bones and buried them under the tamarisk tree in Yavesh, and fasted for seven days.
